its contents may constitute a violation of the Bank\\'s information security policies and applicable law.\n\nRight-click to update Table of Contents\n\n**[Section 1: Executive Summary]{.underline}**\n\nHarborview Capital Partners LLC (the \\\"Borrower\\\") has requested a \\$47,500,000 construction-to-mini-permanent loan from Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. (the \\\"Bank\\\") for the ground-up construction of \\\"The Piedmont at Brightleaf,\\\" a 212-unit Class A mixed-use residential and retail development located at 601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, Durham County, North Carolina 27701. The subject property comprises two parcels (Parcel IDs 0821-04-72-3341 and 0821-04-72-4102) with a combined land area of approximately 3.47 acres. The site is currently improved as vacant land, following the demolition of former tobacco warehouse structures in August 2024 and the completion of environmental remediation in September 2024.\n\nThe proposed development consists of a six-story mixed-use building with five residential floors constructed above a one-story podium containing ground-floor retail space and a four-level structured parking garage. The residential program comprises 212 units: 142 one-bedroom units, 56 two-bedroom units, and 14 three-bedroom units. Ground-floor retail consists of approximately 18,400 square feet across seven retail bays. The structured parking garage provides 276 parking spaces, representing a ratio of approximately 1.30 spaces per residential unit. Total gross building area is approximately 287,500 square feet. Project amenities include a rooftop terrace, fitness center, co-working lounge, pool deck, and dog park. The property is zoned MU-D (Mixed Use --- Downtown), and the proposed development is permitted as of right under the applicable zoning ordinance.\n\nThe Relationship Manager for this credit is David Forsythe, SVP, Commercial Real Estate Lending. A term sheet was executed on January 10, 2025 by David Forsythe on behalf of the Bank and Elena Vasquez-Torres, Managing Member of the Borrower. The target closing date is February 28, 2025. The Bank\\'s outside legal counsel for this transaction is Ashford, Cromdale Consulting & Laine LLP (lead partner Katharine \\\"Kate\\\" Drummond; associate Marcus Webb), located at 411 South Tryon Street, Suite 2800, Charlotte, North Carolina 28202. The Borrower\\'s counsel is Calloway & Strauss PLLC (lead partner Jonathan F. Ngo), located at 324 Blackwell Street, Suite 200, Durham, North Carolina 27701.\n\nThe Commercial Real Estate Lending group recommends **APPROVAL** of this loan, subject to material conditions including, without limitation, (a) resolution of a loan-to-cost ratio exceedance through funding of an equity escrow deposit, (b) execution of an intercreditor agreement with the mezzanine lender, (c) implementation of single-purpose entity covenants and delivery of a non-consolidation opinion, and (d) resolution of other conditions detailed in Section 10 of this memorandum. The loan as structured presents certain underwriting concerns regarding borrower equity contribution and LTC compliance that must be addressed as conditions of closing. Additionally, this credit memorandum identifies several risk factors and structural issues --- including interest reserve adequacy, affiliate relationships between a Borrower member and the mezzanine lender, conflicting membership interest pledges, environmental use restrictions, and title matters --- each of which requires resolution or mitigation before or at closing. These matters are fully analyzed in Sections 5, 6, 9, and 10 of this memorandum.\n\n**[Section 2: Borrower / Sponsor Overview]{.underline}**\n\n**[2.1 Borrower Entity]{.underline}**\n\nThe Borrower is Harborview Capital Partners LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company formed on March 14, 2019 and registered with the North Carolina Secretary of State under File Number 1923847. The Borrower\\'s principal office is located at 400 West Main Street, Suite 310, Durham, North Carolina 27701. The Borrower was formed for the specific purpose of developing and operating The Piedmont at Brightleaf and is intended to function as a single-purpose entity (\\\"SPE\\\") in connection with the proposed financing.\n\nThe Borrower\\'s current net worth, as reflected in its most recent financial statements provided to the Bank, is approximately \\$14,200,000. The proposed loan documentation will include a minimum net worth covenant of \\$12,000,000, to be tested annually within 120 days of the Borrower\\'s fiscal year end. The current net worth provides a cushion of approximately \\$2,200,000 above the covenant minimum.\n\nThe Borrower\\'s principal, Elena Vasquez-Torres, and her affiliated entities have a demonstrated track record in commercial real estate development in the Research Triangle area. Prior to the formation of the Borrower, Ms. Vasquez-Torres and her development team successfully completed four mixed-use projects in the Triangle area, comprising a total of approximately 580 residential units and 65,000 square feet of commercial space, with aggregate development costs of approximately \\$128 million. Each of these projects was completed on time and within budget, and all four have achieved stabilized occupancy levels consistent with or exceeding original projections.\n\nThe Bank\\'s underwriting standards for construction loans require standard SPE covenants, including separateness covenants, restrictions on commingling of funds with affiliates, and prohibition on incurrence of additional indebtedness outside the approved capital structure. Given the affiliate relationship between a Borrower member (Brightleaf Investors Group LLC) and the mezzanine lender (Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC), outside counsel should evaluate whether an independent manager requirement and/or a non-consolidation opinion should be required. This is discussed further in Section 9 (Risk Factors). The Borrower\\'s operating agreement will need to be amended prior to closing to incorporate the required SPE provisions and the independent manager requirement, and the Bank\\'s counsel should review the amended operating agreement to confirm compliance with the Bank\\'s standard form and requirements.\n\n**[2.2 Membership / Organizational Structure]{.underline}**\n\nThe Borrower\\'s membership structure, as set forth in its operating agreement dated March 14, 2019 (as amended), is as follows:\n\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Member**                       **Membership Interest**   **Role**\n  -------------------------------- ------------------------- -----------------------\n  Elena Vasquez-Torres             62%                       Managing Member\n\n  Brightleaf Investors Group LLC   28%                       Member\n\n  Reginald K. Osei                 10%                       Member\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Elena Vasquez-Torres** serves as the Managing Member of the Borrower and has day-to-day management authority over all operations of the Borrower and the Project pursuant to the operating agreement. Ms. Vasquez-Torres has the authority to execute contracts, manage development activities, and conduct the ordinary business affairs of the Borrower without the consent of the other members. Major decisions --- including the incurrence of indebtedness, sale or disposition of the Project, refinancing of existing debt, and any voluntary filing for bankruptcy or insolvency protection --- require the affirmative approval of members holding at least 75% of the outstanding membership interests.\n\n**Brightleaf Investors Group LLC** is a North Carolina limited liability company (NC Secretary of State File No. 2045612) that serves as the investment vehicle for a group of local high-net-worth investors. Its Managing Member is Thomas R. Chen. Notably, Thomas R. Chen also controls Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC (NC Secretary of State File No. 2103988), the entity providing the \\$9,200,000 mezzanine loan for the Project. These two entities --- Brightleaf Investors Group LLC and Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC --- share common control through Mr. Chen, creating a structural affiliate relationship that is discussed extensively in Sections 4.4 and 9.3 of this memorandum. This common control raises concerns regarding the independence of the mezzanine financing from the Borrower\\'s equity structure and introduces substantive consolidation risk that must be addressed through appropriate SPE covenants, independent manager provisions, and a non-consolidation opinion.\n\n**Reginald K. Osei** holds a 10% membership interest in the Borrower and participates as a passive investor and guarantor. Mr. Osei has 15 years of experience in real estate finance and development and contributes financial capacity to the guaranty structure.\n\n**Land Contribution.** The land comprising the Project site was acquired by Ms. Vasquez-Torres on behalf of the Borrower through capital calls from all three members, including Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, at a total acquisition cost of \\$8,200,000. The land was contributed to the Borrower and is currently owned by the Borrower free and clear of liens, except for an unsatisfied deed of trust from Triangle Commercial Bank that is to be paid off at closing (see Section 9.7). The Bank notes that the land acquisition cost of \\$8,200,000 was funded through capital calls from members, including Brightleaf Investors Group LLC. Given that Brightleaf Investors Group LLC is an affiliate of the mezzanine lender, a portion of the \\\"equity\\\" invested in the land may be indirectly sourced from the same investor group providing the mezzanine financing. The Bank\\'s underwriting policy does not count mezzanine debt as borrower equity. Outside counsel should evaluate whether the land contribution satisfies the Bank\\'s minimum equity requirements or whether circular funding concerns exist. This matter is discussed further in Sections 5.1 and 9.1.\n\n**[2.3 Guarantor Profiles]{.underline}**\n\n**2.3.1 Elena Vasquez-Torres**\n\nElena Vasquez-Torres, age 54, is a resident of Durham, North Carolina with over 22 years of commercial real estate development experience in the Research Triangle region. Ms. Vasquez-Torres serves as the Managing Member of the Borrower, holding a 62% membership interest. She has direct oversight of all aspects of the Project\\'s development and will serve as the primary point of contact for the Bank throughout the construction and mini-perm periods.\n\nMs. Vasquez-Torres\\'s personal financial summary, based on the personal financial statement dated December 15, 2024 provided to the Bank, is as follows:\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Category**                                                    **Amount**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Liquid Assets (cash, marketable securities)                     \\$6,100,000\n\n  Real Estate Holdings                                            \\$19,400,000\n\n  Other Assets (business interests, retirement accounts, other)   \\$4,300,000\n\n  **Total Net Worth**                                             **\\$29,800,000**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMs. Vasquez-Torres will provide a completion guaranty, a carry guaranty, and a 25% partial repayment guaranty (25% × \\$47,500,000 = \\$11,875,000). The guaranty includes \\\"bad boy\\\" carve-out provisions that convert the partial repayment guaranty to full recourse upon the occurrence of specified triggering events, including fraud or intentional misrepresentation, voluntary bankruptcy filing, unauthorized transfers of collateral, environmental liability, waste, and violation of SPE covenants.\n\n**2.3.2 Reginald K. Osei**\n\nReginald K. Osei, age 47, is a resident of Chapel Hill, North Carolina with approximately 15 years of experience in real estate finance and development. Mr. Osei holds a 10% membership interest in the Borrower and participates as a financial partner and guarantor.\n\nMr. Osei\\'s personal financial summary, based on the personal financial statement dated December 18, 2024 provided to the Bank, is as follows:\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Category**                                                    **Amount**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Liquid Assets (cash, marketable securities)                     \\$3,200,000\n\n  Real Estate Holdings                                            \\$6,800,000\n\n  Other Assets (business interests, retirement accounts, other)   \\$1,500,000\n\n  **Total Net Worth**                                             **\\$11,500,000**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nMr. Osei will provide a completion guaranty, a carry guaranty, and a 25% partial repayment guaranty (25% × \\$47,500,000 = \\$11,875,000). His guaranty structure mirrors that of Ms. Vasquez-Torres, including the same \\\"bad boy\\\" carve-out provisions converting the partial repayment guaranty to full recourse upon the occurrence of triggering events.\n\n**2.3.3 Combined Guarantor Analysis**\n\nThe combined financial strength of the two guarantors is summarized as follows:\n\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Metric**                            **Amount / Ratio**\n  ------------------------------------- --------------------------------------------\n  Combined Net Worth                    \\$41,300,000 (\\$29,800,000 + \\$11,500,000)\n\n  Combined Liquidity                    \\$9,300,000 (\\$6,100,000 + \\$3,200,000)\n\n  Minimum Combined Liquidity Covenant   \\$5,000,000 (tested quarterly)\n\n  Liquidity Coverage Ratio              1.86x (\\$9,300,000 ÷ \\$5,000,000)\n\n  Liquidity Cushion Above Minimum       \\$4,300,000\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nEach guarantor\\'s partial repayment guaranty of \\$11,875,000 represents approximately 39.8% of Ms. Vasquez-Torres\\'s net worth and approximately 103.3% of Mr. Osei\\'s net worth. The Bank notes that Mr. Osei\\'s guaranty exposure is high relative to his personal net worth, and his ability to satisfy the full amount of his partial repayment guaranty in a default scenario is dependent on the value of his illiquid real estate holdings. The combined guarantor financial strength is assessed as adequate but concentrated in illiquid real estate assets. Both guarantors\\' net worth is heavily weighted toward real estate holdings, which could be subject to valuation declines in a market downturn. The liquidity cushion above the covenant minimum (\\$4,300,000) provides a reasonable but not excessive buffer.\n\n**[Section 3: Project Description and Market Analysis]{.underline}**\n\n**[3.1 Project Description]{.underline}**\n\nThe Piedmont at Brightleaf is a proposed Class A mixed-use residential and retail development to be constructed at 601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, North Carolina 27701. The Project site comprises two contiguous parcels identified as Durham County Parcel IDs 0821-04-72-3341 and 0821-04-72-4102, with a combined land area of approximately 3.47 acres. The site is located in the Brightleaf Historic District of downtown Durham, within walking distance of the American Tobacco Campus, Durham Performing Arts Center, and Durham Bulls Athletic Park.\n\nThe site is currently improved as vacant land. The former tobacco warehouse structures that previously occupied the parcels were demolished in August 2024, and environmental remediation activities (relating to petroleum contamination from a former underground storage tank) were completed in September 2024. The North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality (\\\"NCDEQ\\\") issued a No Further Action (\\\"NFA\\\") letter dated September 30, 2024 for commercial use of the site, subject to certain conditions described in Section 8.2 of this memorandum.\n\nThe proposed development program is as follows:\n\n> • **Building Configuration:** Six-story mixed-use structure consisting of five residential floors constructed above a one-story podium containing ground-floor retail space and the upper levels of a four-level structured parking garage.\n>\n> • **Residential Units:** 212 units total --- 142 one-bedroom units, 56 two-bedroom units, and 14 three-bedroom units.\n>\n> • **Retail Space:** Approximately 18,400 square feet of ground-floor retail across seven retail bays, with street-facing frontage along Foster Street and a secondary entrance from the interior courtyard.\n>\n> • **Structured Parking:** Four-level garage providing 276 parking spaces, representing a ratio of approximately 1.30 spaces per residential unit. Parking will include both assigned resident spaces and unassigned visitor and retail patron spaces.\n>\n> • **Total Gross Building Area:** Approximately 287,500 square feet.\n>\n> • **Amenities:** Rooftop terrace with outdoor seating, gas grills, and fire pit areas; fitness center (approximately 3,200 square feet); co-working lounge with private offices and conference room; resort-style pool deck with cabanas; and enclosed dog park.\n\nThe general contractor is Graystone Construction Group Inc. (North Carolina General Contractor License No. 78442), located at 1200 Fayetteville Road, Raleigh, North Carolina 27603. The president of Graystone Construction Group Inc. is Mark D. Sullivan. The construction contract is structured as a Guaranteed Maximum Price (\\\"GMP\\\") contract with a total GMP of \\$34,750,000. The architect of record is Meridian Design Collective PC, a North Carolina professional corporation. Projected substantial completion is August 31, 2027. The property is zoned MU-D (Mixed Use --- Downtown) under the Durham Unified Development Ordinance, and the proposed development is permitted as of right without the need for any zoning variance, special use permit, or rezoning.\n\n**[3.2 Market Overview]{.underline}**\n\nThe Durham, North Carolina multifamily market continues to demonstrate strong fundamentals driven by the area\\'s robust economic base. The Research Triangle region --- anchored by Duke University, the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill, and North Carolina State University --- has attracted significant investment in biotechnology, pharmaceutical research, information technology, and financial services. Downtown Durham, in particular, has experienced substantial revitalization over the past decade, with the American Tobacco Campus, Durham Innovation District, and the expanding Duke University Health System serving as major employment and demand drivers. Population growth in Durham County has averaged approximately 1.8% annually over the past five years, and the median household income in the downtown core has increased by over 15% during the same period.\n\nAverage market rents for Class A multifamily product in downtown Durham currently range from approximately \\$1,950 to \\$2,500 per month for one-bedroom units and \\$2,300 to \\$3,200 per month for two-bedroom units, depending on unit size, finishes, and amenity package. The Project\\'s projected average rent of \\$2,245 per month across all unit types is positioned in the mid-range of the competitive set and is supported by comparable lease data from properties including the Van Alen, One City Center Apartments, and Beckon on Geer Street. The Bank considers this rent projection to be reasonable and achievable based on current market conditions.\n\nGround-floor retail rents in downtown Durham for Class A mixed-use buildings with street-level visibility range from approximately \\$26.00 to \\$35.00 per square foot on a triple-net basis. The projected retail rent of \\$30.00 per square foot NNN is within this market range and is considered reasonable given the Project\\'s location and visibility along Foster Street.\n\nDurham Class A multifamily vacancy rates have historically ranged from approximately 4% to 7%, with the most recent data indicating a vacancy rate of approximately 5.2% for comparable properties. The Project\\'s underwritten vacancy assumption of 5% is consistent with historical trends and is considered appropriate. While there is a notable pipeline of competing multifamily projects under development or planned in the greater Durham area, market demand continues to absorb new supply at a rate that supports the Project\\'s lease-up assumptions. The Bank\\'s independent market assessment supports the appraised values discussed in Section 3.3.\n\n**[3.3 Appraisal Summary]{.underline}**\n\nAn appraisal of the subject property was prepared by Linda M. Haverford, MAI, of Ridgeline Appraisal Services Inc., Raleigh, North Carolina, dated November 15, 2024. The appraisal was prepared in compliance with the Uniform Standards of Professional Appraisal Practice (\\\"USPAP\\\") and meets the requirements of the Financial Institutions Reform, Recovery, and Enforcement Act of 1989 (\\\"FIRREA\\\"). The appraisal utilized the sales comparison approach for the as-is land value and the income capitalization and sales comparison approaches for the as-completed and as-stabilized valuations. The appraisal conclusions are as follows:\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Valuation Basis**                           **Appraised Value**\n  --------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  As-Is Land Value                              \\$8,900,000\n\n  As-Completed Value                            \\$72,800,000\n\n  As-Stabilized Value (assumes 95% occupancy)   \\$76,200,000\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe appraiser\\'s projected stabilized net operating income is \\$4,876,800 per annum, derived as follows:\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Income / Expense Category**                                       **Amount**\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Residential Rental Income (212 units × \\$2,245 avg. monthly × 12)   \\$5,712,000\n\n  Retail Rental Income (18,400 SF × \\$30.00/SF NNN)                   \\$552,000\n\n  Other Income (parking, amenities, miscellaneous)                    \\$198,000\n\n  **Gross Potential Income**                                          **\\$6,462,000**\n\n  Less: Vacancy & Credit Loss (5.0%)                                  (\\$323,100)\n\n  **Effective Gross Income**                                          **\\$6,138,900**\n\n  Less: Operating Expenses                                            (\\$1,262,100)\n\n  **Stabilized Net Operating Income**                                 **\\$4,876,800**\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe implied capitalization rate on the as-stabilized value is \\$4,876,800 ÷ \\$76,200,000 = 6.40%, which is consistent with current market capitalization rates for comparable Class A mixed-use assets in the Durham market. The Bank has reviewed the appraisal and considers the appraised values reasonable and well-supported by the comparable sales data, income approach analysis, and market conditions presented by the appraiser.\n\n**[Section 4: Loan Terms and Structure]{.underline}**\n\n**[4.1 Summary of Proposed Terms]{.underline}**\n\nThe following table summarizes the material terms of the proposed loan facility as set forth in the executed term sheet dated January 10, 2025:\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Term**                            **Description**\n  ----------------------------------- -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Loan Amount**                     \\$47,500,000\n\n  **Loan Type**                       Construction-to-Mini-Permanent\n\n  **Construction Period**             30 months from closing (closing target: February 28, 2025; construction maturity: August 28, 2027)\n\n  **Mini-Perm Period**                12 months following construction maturity (mini-perm maturity: August 28, 2028)\n\n  **Extension Option**                One 6-month extension (extending maturity to February 28, 2029), subject to conditions to be specified in loan documentation (see Section 11)\n\n  **Interest Rate (Construction)**    Daily Simple SOFR + 3.25% per annum; SOFR floor of 1.00%\n\n  **Interest Rate (Mini-Perm)**       Daily Simple SOFR + 3.50% per annum; SOFR floor of 1.00%\n\n  **Default Rate**                    Contract rate + 5.00% per annum\n\n  **Payments**                        Interest-only, paid monthly in arrears, during both construction and mini-perm periods\n\n  **Origination Fee**                 1.00% × \\$47,500,000 = \\$475,000, payable at closing\n\n  **Exit Fee**                        0.50% of outstanding principal if repaid during construction period; 0.25% if repaid during first 6 months of mini-perm; no exit fee thereafter\n\n  **Unused Line Fee**                 0.25% per annum on average daily undrawn balance\n\n  **Interest Reserve**                \\$4,850,000 funded from loan proceeds at closing, held in a designated sub-account\n\n  **Retainage**                       10% holdback on hard construction costs, released post-certificate of occupancy\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe interest rate during the construction period, based on the current Daily Simple SOFR rate of approximately 4.35% as of the date of this memorandum, is approximately 7.60% (4.35% + 3.25%). During the mini-perm period, the all-in rate would be approximately 7.85% (4.35% + 3.50%). These rates are subject to change based on movements in SOFR. The SOFR floor of 1.00% ensures a minimum all-in rate of 4.25% during the construction period and 4.50% during the mini-perm period, regardless of declines in SOFR.\n\n**[4.2 Collateral Package]{.underline}**\n\nThe proposed loan will be secured by the following collateral:\n\n1\\. **First Priority Deed of Trust** on the Property, encumbering both parcels (0821-04-72-3341 and 0821-04-72-4102), together with all improvements, appurtenances, and related property rights.\n\n2\\. **First Priority Assignment of Rents and Leases** covering all existing and future leases, rents, income, and profits from the Property.\n\n3\\. **First Priority Security Interest** in all personal property, fixtures, equipment, permits, plans, specifications, contracts, and other intangible property related to the Project, to be perfected by filing a UCC-1 Financing Statement with the North Carolina Secretary of State.\n\n4\\. **Assignment of Construction Contract** --- collateral assignment of the GMP contract with Graystone Construction Group Inc.\n\n5\\. **Assignment of Architect\\'s Contract** --- collateral assignment of the architect\\'s agreement with Meridian Design Collective PC.\n\n6\\. **Collateral Assignment of Insurance Proceeds and Condemnation Awards** --- assignment of all insurance proceeds and condemnation or eminent domain awards relating to the Property.\n\n7\\. **Pledge of Membership Interests** --- pledge of 100% of the membership interests in the Borrower by all members (Elena Vasquez-Torres, Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, and Reginald K. Osei).\n\nRegarding the membership interest pledge, the Bank notes a material conflict: the term sheet includes a pledge of 100% of membership interests in the Borrower as collateral for the senior loan. However, the mezzanine loan from Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC (\\$9,200,000) is also secured by a pledge of 100% of the membership interests. These pledges are mutually exclusive as first-priority security interests. Resolution through the intercreditor agreement is a required condition of closing. Specifically, the closing must be conditioned on execution of an intercreditor agreement with Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC that provides the Bank with a first-priority pledge and the mezzanine lender with a second-priority pledge of the membership interests. The Bank recommends this approach (option (b)) as the preferred resolution. Alternatively, the senior lender could forego the membership interest pledge and rely solely on the real property collateral, or the mezzanine lender could subordinate its pledge entirely. However, option (b) preserves the Bank\\'s maximum collateral coverage while permitting the mezzanine lender to retain a subordinated security position. See Section 4.4 (Mezzanine Debt / Intercreditor) and Section 9 (Risk Factors) for further discussion.\n\n**[4.3 Guaranty Structure]{.underline}**\n\nThe proposed guaranty structure is as follows:\n\n**Guarantors:** Elena Vasquez-Torres and Reginald K. Osei.\n\n**Completion Guaranty.** Both guarantors will jointly and severally guarantee the lien-free completion of construction of the Project in accordance with the approved plans and specifications and the approved construction budget, on or before the construction maturity date. The completion guaranty obligates the guarantors to fund any cost overruns beyond the approved budget and contingency amounts, and to cause the Project to achieve substantial completion and obtain all required certificates of occupancy.\n\n**Carry Guaranty.** Both guarantors will jointly and severally guarantee payment of all carrying costs during the construction period, including monthly interest payments, real estate taxes, insurance premiums, and other carrying expenses as specified in the loan documentation.\n\n**Partial Repayment Guaranty.** Each guarantor will provide a 25% partial repayment guaranty --- 25% of the loan amount of \\$47,500,000 = \\$11,875,000 per guarantor. The partial repayment guaranty is a payment guaranty obligating each guarantor to pay the Bank up to \\$11,875,000 in the event of a payment default or other event of default under the loan.\n\n**\\\"Bad Boy\\\" Carve-Outs.** The partial repayment guaranty will convert to a full recourse guaranty (100% of the outstanding principal balance plus accrued interest, fees, and costs) upon the occurrence of specified triggering events, including: fraud or intentional misrepresentation by the Borrower or any guarantor; voluntary filing for bankruptcy, reorganization, or insolvency protection by the Borrower; unauthorized transfers or encumbrances of the Property or membership interests; environmental contamination caused by the Borrower; violation of SPE covenants; and waste or destruction of the collateral. All guaranties will survive any foreclosure, deed-in-lieu of foreclosure, or other realization upon the collateral.\n\n**North Carolina Considerations. The Bank notes that North Carolina is a judicial foreclosure state and that deficiency judgments following foreclosure are subject to the fair price confirmation hearing requirements of N.C.G.S. § 45-21.36. The Bank\\'s outside counsel (Ashford, Cromdale Consulting & Laine LLP) should review the guaranty structure for compliance with North Carolina law, including the enforceability of the guaranty obligations independent of the underlying indebtedness and the effect of anti-deficiency protections on guarantor liability.**\n\n**[4.4 Mezzanine Debt / Intercreditor Arrangement]{.underline}**\n\nThe Borrower has arranged mezzanine financing from Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company (NC Secretary of State File No. 2103988), to supplement the senior construction loan and fund a portion of the Project\\'s total development costs. The material terms of the mezzanine financing, as provided to the Bank, are summarized below:\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Term**                            **Description**\n  ----------------------------------- -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Mezzanine Lender**                Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC\n\n  **Mezzanine Loan Amount**           \\$9,200,000\n\n  **Interest Rate**                   12.50% fixed per annum\n\n  **Maturity**                        Coterminous with senior loan (including any extensions)\n\n  **Collateral**                      Pledge of 100% of membership interests in the Borrower\n\n  **Payments**                        Interest accrues and is paid-in-kind during construction; cash pay during mini-perm\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe intercreditor agreement between the Bank and Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC is currently in the process of being negotiated by the respective parties\\' legal counsel, but has not yet been executed as of the date of this credit memorandum. The intercreditor agreement must be in form and substance satisfactory to the Bank and its counsel (Ashford, Cromdale Consulting & Laine LLP). At a minimum, the intercreditor agreement must contain the following provisions:\n\n> • The mezzanine lender\\'s pledge of membership interests shall be subordinated to the senior lender\\'s first-priority pledge, such that the senior lender holds a first-priority security interest and the mezzanine lender holds a second-priority security interest in the membership interests.\n>\n> • A standstill period of not less than 90 days following the occurrence of a default under the mezzanine loan, during which the mezzanine lender may not exercise any remedies against the Borrower, the membership interests, or the Project.\n>\n> • Notice and cure rights for the mezzanine lender, permitting the mezzanine lender to receive copies of default notices issued by the senior lender and to cure defaults under the senior loan within a specified cure period.\n>\n> • A mezzanine lender purchase option, granting the mezzanine lender the right (but not the obligation) to purchase the senior loan from the Bank at par plus all accrued and unpaid interest, fees, and costs.\n>\n> • A prohibition on the mezzanine lender modifying any material term of the mezzanine loan (including maturity, interest rate, or collateral) without the prior written consent of the senior lender.\n>\n> • Bankruptcy-related restrictions, including a prohibition on the mezzanine lender filing or joining in the filing of an involuntary bankruptcy petition against the Borrower.\n>\n> • Acknowledgment by the mezzanine lender that it will not contest, challenge, or interfere with the Bank\\'s exercise of remedies under the senior loan documents.\n\n**Affiliate Relationship.** As noted in Section 2.2, Thomas R. Chen is the managing member of both Brightleaf Investors Group LLC (which holds a 28% membership interest in the Borrower) and Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC (the mezzanine lender). This common control creates a structural relationship between the mezzanine lender and a significant equity holder of the Borrower that must be carefully addressed. The intercreditor agreement should include express acknowledgments of this affiliate relationship and provisions designed to prevent the mezzanine lender from leveraging its affiliate\\'s equity position to the detriment of the senior lender. The Bank does not count the mezzanine loan as borrower equity for purposes of LTC calculation. The mezzanine loan is structurally subordinated debt, not equity.\n\nThe execution of the intercreditor agreement is a mandatory condition precedent to closing and may not be waived. The Bank will not fund any advance under the construction loan until the intercreditor agreement has been fully executed and delivered by all parties.\n\n**[Section 5: Sources and Uses / Project Budget]{.underline}**\n\n**[5.1 Sources of Funds]{.underline}**\n\nThe total development cost of the Project is \\$56,700,000. The proposed sources of funds are as follows:\n\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Source**                                               **Amount**              **% of Total**\n  -------------------------------------------------------- ----------------------- -----------------------\n  Senior Construction Loan (Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A.)   \\$47,500,000            83.77%\n\n  Mezzanine Loan (Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC)        \\$9,200,000             16.23%\n\n  Borrower Cash Equity                                     \\$0                     0.00%\n\n  **Total Sources**                                        **\\$56,700,000**        **100.00%**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**The Borrower is contributing \\$0 in direct cash equity to the Project.** The land acquisition cost of \\$8,200,000 was funded from prior capital calls from members, including contributions from Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, which is affiliated with the mezzanine lender, Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC. All remaining project costs are funded through the senior construction loan and the mezzanine loan. Per the Bank\\'s underwriting policy, mezzanine debt is not counted as borrower equity.\n\nThe LTC calculation presents a compliance issue. Total project costs equal \\$56,700,000. The senior loan amount is \\$47,500,000. Therefore, LTC = \\$47,500,000 ÷ \\$56,700,000 = **83.77%**. The Bank\\'s maximum permitted LTC per the executed term sheet and per the Bank\\'s Commercial Real Estate Lending Policy is 80%. At the maximum LTC of 80%, the maximum permitted senior loan amount would be \\$56,700,000 × 80% = \\$45,360,000. The proposed loan amount of \\$47,500,000 exceeds this maximum by \\$2,140,000. This discrepancy must be resolved prior to closing through the equity escrow mechanism described in Section 5.3 and conditioned in Section 10.\n\nAdditionally, the Bank notes that the Borrower\\'s only equity contribution to the Project is the previously acquired land, which has an as-is appraised value of \\$8,900,000 and was acquired for \\$8,200,000. While the land value exceeds the \\$2,140,000 shortfall, a portion of the capital used to acquire the land was sourced from Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, an affiliate of the mezzanine lender. This creates a potential circular funding concern. The Senior Loan Committee requires, as a condition of closing, that the Borrower either: (a) deposit \\$2,140,000 in cash into a Bank-controlled equity escrow account prior to closing; or (b) reduce the loan amount to \\$45,360,000; or (c) provide additional evidence satisfactory to the Bank that the land equity is independent of the mezzanine financing.\n\n**[5.2 Uses of Funds]{.underline}**\n\nThe total development budget for the Project, as supported by the executed GMP contract and the Borrower\\'s detailed budget, is as follows:\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Use**                                                               **Amount**              **% of Total**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------- ----------------------- -----------------------\n  Land Acquisition (previously closed, funded from borrower equity)     \\$8,200,000             14.46%\n\n  Hard Construction Costs (GMP --- Graystone Construction Group Inc.)   \\$34,750,000            61.29%\n\n  Soft Costs (architecture, engineering, permits, legal, accounting)    \\$4,150,000             7.32%\n\n  Interest Reserve                                                      \\$4,850,000             8.55%\n\n  Loan Origination Fee (1.00% × \\$47,500,000)                           \\$475,000               0.84%\n\n  Developer Fee (5.0% of total project cost)                            \\$2,835,000             5.00%\n\n  Contingency                                                           \\$1,440,000             2.54%\n\n  **Total Uses**                                                        **\\$56,700,000**        **100.00%**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Reconciliation:** \\$8,200,000 + \\$34,750,000 + \\$4,150,000 + \\$4,850,000 + \\$475,000 + \\$2,835,000 + \\$1,440,000 = \\$56,700,000.\n\nThe developer fee of \\$2,835,000 represents 5.0% of total project costs, which is within the range of market norms for a development of this size and complexity (typically 3% to 6%). The contingency of \\$1,440,000 represents approximately 4.1% of hard construction costs (\\$1,440,000 ÷ \\$34,750,000 = 4.14%), which is adequate for a ground-up mixed-use construction project of this type. The Bank\\'s policy generally recommends a minimum contingency of 3% to 5% of hard construction costs for new construction projects.\n\n**[5.3 Loan-to-Cost Analysis]{.underline}**\n\nThe loan-to-cost ratio is a critical underwriting metric that measures the Bank\\'s exposure relative to total project costs. The detailed LTC calculation is as follows:\n\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Metric**                                               **Amount / Ratio**\n  -------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Total Project Costs                                      \\$56,700,000\n\n  Senior Loan Amount                                       \\$47,500,000\n\n  **LTC Ratio**                                            **83.77%**\n\n  Maximum Permitted LTC (per term sheet and Bank policy)   80.00%\n\n  **Excess**                                               **3.77 percentage points**\n\n  Maximum Loan at 80% LTC                                  \\$45,360,000\n\n  **Dollar Shortfall**                                     **\\$2,140,000**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe LTC ratio of 83.77% exceeds the Bank\\'s maximum of 80% as stated in the term sheet. The term sheet was executed with a loan amount of \\$47,500,000 and total project costs of \\$56,700,000 without reconciling the LTC covenant. This discrepancy must be resolved prior to closing. The Senior Loan Committee acknowledges the LTC exceedance and approves the loan subject to the condition that the Borrower fund an equity escrow deposit of not less than \\$2,140,000 in cash (from sources other than the mezzanine loan or any affiliate of the mezzanine lender) into a Bank-controlled escrow account prior to or at closing. This equity escrow will be applied to project costs first, ahead of loan advances, to bring the effective LTC into compliance.\n\nFor additional context, the Bank notes the following alternative analysis: If the mezzanine debt of \\$9,200,000 were hypothetically counted as equity (which the Bank\\'s underwriting policy does not permit), the combined \\\"equity\\\" in the capital structure would be \\$9,200,000 and the senior loan LTC would remain unchanged at 83.77%. The combined senior and mezzanine leverage is (\\$47,500,000 + \\$9,200,000) ÷ \\$56,700,000 = 100%, meaning there is zero true equity cushion in the capital structure absent the land value. This analysis underscores the importance of the equity escrow requirement and the need for the Bank to confirm that the land equity is genuinely independent of the mezzanine financing.\n\nEven with the proposed equity escrow of \\$2,140,000, the Borrower\\'s total equity contribution to the Project would consist of \\$2,140,000 in cash plus \\$8,200,000 in land (at cost), totaling \\$10,340,000 out of \\$56,700,000 in total project costs, or approximately 18.2%. While this level of equity is relatively thin for a ground-up construction loan, the Bank takes comfort in the strong LTV ratios (65.25% as-completed, 62.34% as-stabilized) and the combined guarantor net worth of \\$41,300,000 as additional credit support.\n\n**[Section 6: Financial Projections and Underwriting Metrics]{.underline}**\n\n**[6.1 Key Underwriting Ratios]{.underline}**\n\nThe following table summarizes the key underwriting metrics for the proposed loan:\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Metric**                     **Calculation**               **Result**     **Bank Policy**    **Status**\n  ------------------------------ ----------------------------- -------------- ------------------ --------------\n  LTV (As-Completed)             \\$47,500,000 ÷ \\$72,800,000   65.25%         Max 70%            Pass\n\n  LTV (As-Stabilized)            \\$47,500,000 ÷ \\$76,200,000   62.34%         Max 70%            Pass\n\n  LTC                            \\$47,500,000 ÷ \\$56,700,000   83.77%         Max 80%            **Fail**\n\n  DSCR (Stabilized, I/O)         \\$4,876,800 ÷ \\$3,728,750     1.31x          Min 1.25x          Pass\n\n  DSCR (Stress Test, +200 bps)   \\$4,876,800 ÷ \\$4,678,750     1.04x          Min 1.25x          **Fail**\n\n  Borrower Net Worth             \\$14,200,000                  ---            Min \\$12,000,000   Pass\n\n  Combined Guarantor Liquidity   \\$9,300,000                   ---            Min \\$5,000,000    Pass\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Projected DSCR (Stabilized, Interest-Only).** The stabilized DSCR is calculated using the appraiser\\'s projected stabilized NOI of \\$4,876,800 and the annual interest expense on the fully funded loan amount at the mini-perm rate. Assuming a Daily Simple SOFR rate of 4.35% (as of the credit memo date) plus the mini-perm spread of 3.50%, the all-in rate is 7.85%. Annual interest on \\$47,500,000 at 7.85% = \\$3,728,750. DSCR = \\$4,876,800 ÷ \\$3,728,750 = **1.31x**, which exceeds the Bank\\'s minimum of 1.25x.\n\n**Stress-Tested DSCR.** Under a stress scenario assuming a 200-basis-point increase in SOFR (to 6.35%), the all-in mini-perm rate would be 9.85% (6.35% + 3.50%). Annual interest on \\$47,500,000 at 9.85% = \\$4,678,750. Stressed DSCR = \\$4,876,800 ÷ \\$4,678,750 = **1.04x**, which falls below the Bank\\'s minimum of 1.25x. This stress test illustrates the loan\\'s sensitivity to interest rate increases and underscores the importance of the Borrower securing permanent take-out financing or obtaining an interest rate cap prior to the commencement of the mini-perm period or any extension thereof. The Bank should consider requiring an interest rate cap as a condition of the extension option.\n\n**[6.2 Interest Reserve Analysis]{.underline}**\n\nThe interest reserve of \\$4,850,000 is funded from loan proceeds at closing and held in a designated sub-account to cover monthly interest payments during the construction period. The adequacy of this reserve is a critical underwriting consideration, as depletion of the reserve before construction completion would require the Borrower to fund interest from its own resources.\n\n**Current Rate Assumptions.** The current Daily Simple SOFR rate is approximately 4.35% as of the date of this memorandum. The construction period all-in rate is Daily Simple SOFR + 3.25% = 7.60%.\n\n**Full-Draw Scenario.** If the entire \\$47,500,000 were drawn immediately at closing, the annual interest expense would be \\$47,500,000 × 7.60% = \\$3,610,000. Over the 30-month construction period, total interest cost would be approximately \\$3,610,000 × (30 ÷ 12) = \\$9,025,000. However, this scenario is unrealistic because the loan is drawn incrementally over the construction period based on milestone achievements.\n\n**Incremental Draw Scenario.** Based on the draw schedule (Section 7.1), the loan is drawn progressively as construction milestones are achieved. Draw 1 at closing (\\$9,475,000) represents approximately 20% of the total loan commitment. Subsequent draws bring cumulative outstanding balances to progressively higher levels, with Draw 8 (substantial completion) bringing cumulative draws to approximately \\$44,225,000 and the final Draw 9 (retainage release) occurring post-certificate of occupancy. Assuming a roughly linear draw pattern over approximately 18 months to reach the full commitment level (with the remaining 12 months of the construction period at or near full draw), the average outstanding balance over the 30-month construction period is estimated at approximately \\$28,500,000 (roughly 60% of \\$47,500,000).\n\nUsing this average balance, the estimated total interest cost during the 30-month construction period is: \\$28,500,000 × 7.60% × (30 ÷ 12) = approximately **\\$5,415,000**.\n\nThe interest reserve of \\$4,850,000 is therefore approximately **\\$565,000 short** of the estimated total interest cost under current rate assumptions.\n\n**Sensitivity Analysis.** If SOFR increases by 100 basis points to 5.35%, the all-in construction rate would be 8.60%. Estimated total interest cost would increase to \\$28,500,000 × 8.60% × (30 ÷ 12) = approximately **\\$6,128,000**, creating a potential shortfall of approximately \\$1,278,000.\n\nFurthermore, the draw schedule is somewhat front-loaded, with the initial closing draw of \\$9,475,000 representing approximately 20% of the total commitment. This front-loading means the average outstanding balance may be higher than the linear assumption suggests, potentially increasing the total interest cost and widening the reserve shortfall.\n\n**Assessment.** The interest reserve of \\$4,850,000 is **tight** under current rate assumptions and would be clearly inadequate in a rising rate environment. The Senior Loan Committee approves the interest reserve of \\$4,850,000 as adequate based on current rate assumptions, but notes that this amount is at the lower boundary of adequacy. The loan documentation must include the following protective provisions:\n\n> • A mandatory borrower replenishment obligation requiring the Borrower to fund any interest reserve shortfall from its own funds (not from loan proceeds or mezzanine loan proceeds) upon demand by the Bank.\n>\n> • An interest reserve adequacy test to be performed at each draw request, comparing the remaining interest reserve balance against projected interest costs through the earlier of (a) the next anticipated draw date or (b) 90 days. If the projected costs exceed the remaining reserve balance, the Borrower must deposit additional funds into the interest reserve sub-account before the requested advance will be funded.\n>\n> • A reporting obligation requiring the Borrower to provide the Bank with a monthly reconciliation of the interest reserve balance, actual interest charges, and projected remaining interest costs through construction maturity.\n\n**[6.3 Projected Stabilized Cash Flow]{.underline}**\n\nThe Bank has independently verified the appraiser\\'s projected stabilized net operating income by reviewing comparable market data, lease comparables, and operating expense benchmarks for Class A mixed-use properties in the Durham market. The Bank\\'s assessment of the stabilized cash flow is as follows:\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Category**                                                        **Annual Amount**\n  ------------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Residential Rental Income (212 units × \\$2,245 avg. monthly × 12)   \\$5,712,000\n\n  Retail Rental Income (18,400 SF × \\$30.00/SF NNN)                   \\$552,000\n\n  Other Income (parking, amenities, miscellaneous)                    \\$198,000\n\n  **Gross Potential Income**                                          **\\$6,462,000**\n\n  Less: Vacancy & Credit Loss (5.0%)                                  (\\$323,100)\n\n  **Effective Gross Income**                                          **\\$6,138,900**\n\n  Less: Operating Expenses                                            (\\$1,262,100)\n\n  **Stabilized Net Operating Income**                                 **\\$4,876,800**\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe Bank\\'s independent assessment confirms that the NOI assumptions are reasonable and supported by market comparables. The operating expense ratio of approximately 20.6% of effective gross income (\\$1,262,100 ÷ \\$6,138,900) is within the expected range for a Class A mixed-use property with structured parking, which typically exhibits operating expense ratios of 18% to 24% of EGI. The implied capitalization rate of \\$4,876,800 ÷ \\$76,200,000 = 6.40% is consistent with current market capitalization rates for comparable stabilized assets in the Durham market, which generally range from 5.75% to 6.75%.\n\n**[Section 7: Disbursement Schedule and Construction Oversight]{.underline}**\n\n**[7.1 Draw Schedule]{.underline}**\n\nThe proposed draw schedule for the construction loan, based on construction milestones and the approved project budget, is as follows:\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Draw**          **Milestone**                                  **Cumulative % Complete**   **Draw Amount**\n  ----------------- ---------------------------------------------- --------------------------- ------------------\n  1                 Closing (soft costs, interest reserve, fees)   0%                          \\$9,475,000\n\n  2                 Foundation Complete                            15%                         \\$5,212,500\n\n  3                 Structural Frame --- Level 3                   35%                         \\$6,950,000\n\n  4                 Structural Frame --- Topped Out                55%                         \\$6,950,000\n\n  5                 Building Envelope Complete                     70%                         \\$5,212,500\n\n  6                 MEP Rough-In Complete                          80%                         \\$3,475,000\n\n  7                 Interior Finish --- 50% of Units               90%                         \\$3,475,000\n\n  8                 Substantial Completion / TCO                   100%                        \\$3,475,000\n\n  9                 Retainage Release (Post-CO)                    Post-CO                     \\$3,275,000\n\n  **Total**                                                                                    **\\$47,500,000**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Reconciliation:** \\$9,475,000 + \\$5,212,500 + \\$6,950,000 + \\$6,950,000 + \\$5,212,500 + \\$3,475,000 + \\$3,475,000 + \\$3,475,000 + \\$3,275,000 = \\$47,500,000.\n\nDraw 1 at closing includes the following cost components: soft costs (\\$4,150,000), interest reserve (\\$4,850,000), and origination fee (\\$475,000), totaling \\$9,475,000. The 10% retainage holdback on hard construction costs is accumulated throughout the construction period and released as Draw 9 upon satisfaction of retainage release conditions (see Section 7.2 below). The retainage amount of \\$3,275,000 represents approximately 9.4% of the hard construction cost GMP of \\$34,750,000.\n\n**[7.2 Construction Monitoring and Controls]{.underline}**\n\nThe Bank will implement the following construction monitoring procedures and draw controls throughout the construction period:\n\n**Monthly Reporting.** The Borrower is required to provide monthly construction progress reports to the Bank, including updated budget reconciliations, schedule updates, and any material change orders or claims. Each monthly report must be accompanied by a certification from the Borrower\\'s authorized representative that no default exists under the loan documents.\n\n**Third-Party Construction Inspector.** The Bank will engage a third-party construction inspection firm (to be selected prior to closing) to independently verify milestone completion and construction progress prior to the approval of each draw request. The inspector will conduct physical site inspections, review construction documentation, and provide written reports to the Bank confirming the percentage of completion and the adequacy of funds remaining in the budget to complete the Project.\n\n**Draw Request Requirements.** Each draw request submitted by the Borrower must be accompanied by the following documentation:\n\n> • AIA Application for Payment (Forms G702 and G703) certified by the architect of record and the general contractor.\n>\n> • Third-party construction inspector\\'s report confirming achievement of the applicable construction milestone.\n>\n> • Partial lien waivers from the general contractor, all subcontractors, and all materialmen for work performed and materials supplied through the prior draw period.\n>\n> • Updated title endorsement (date-down endorsement) from Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. confirming no intervening liens, encumbrances, or title defects since the prior draw.\n>\n> • Evidence of continued compliance with all insurance requirements.\n>\n> • Certification by the Borrower that no default or event of default exists and that all representations and warranties remain true and correct in all material respects.\n>\n> • Updated budget reconciliation demonstrating that remaining loan proceeds and borrower equity (if applicable) are sufficient to complete the Project.\n\n**Retainage Release Conditions (Draw 9).** Release of the retainage holdback is conditioned upon: (a) issuance of a final certificate of occupancy by the City of Durham; (b) delivery of final, unconditional lien waivers from the general contractor and all subcontractors and materialmen; (c) delivery of the architect\\'s certificate of final completion; and (d) expiration of the statutory lien filing period under N.C.G.S. Chapter 44A (120-day lien filing window from the date of last furnishing of labor or materials) or, alternatively, posting of a lien bond in form and amount satisfactory to the Bank.\n\n**Equity First.** The Borrower\\'s equity (including the equity escrow deposit, if required per Section 5.3) must be fully invested in the Project before any loan advances are made, other than the initial closing advance (Draw 1). This ensures that the Borrower\\'s equity is at risk before the Bank\\'s capital.\n\n**[Section 8: Insurance and Environmental]{.underline}**\n\n**[8.1 Insurance Requirements]{.underline}**\n\nThe Borrower is required to obtain and maintain the following insurance coverages throughout the construction period and the mini-perm period, with all policies in form and substance satisfactory to the Bank:\n\n> • **Builder\\'s Risk Insurance:** All-risk builder\\'s risk insurance covering the full replacement value of the improvements under construction, including materials in transit and materials stored off-site. The policy must name Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. as loss payee.\n>\n> • **Commercial General Liability:** Minimum coverage of \\$5,000,000 per occurrence and \\$10,000,000 in the aggregate, naming Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. as additional insured.\n>\n> • **Umbrella / Excess Liability:** Minimum coverage of \\$10,000,000, in excess of the underlying commercial general liability and employer\\'s liability coverages.\n>\n> • **Workers\\' Compensation:** Statutory limits as required under North Carolina law (N.C.G.S. Chapter 97).\n>\n> • **Flood Insurance:** Required if any portion of the Property is located within a Special Flood Hazard Area as designated by FEMA. A flood zone determination will be obtained prior to closing, and flood insurance will be required to the extent applicable.\n>\n> • **Professional Liability:** The architect (Meridian Design Collective PC) is required to maintain professional liability (errors and omissions) insurance with minimum coverage of \\$2,000,000.\n\nAll insurance policies must remain in full force and effect throughout the term of the loan, including any extension period. Certificates of insurance must be delivered to the Bank prior to closing and updated annually or as policies renew.\n\n**[8.2 Environmental Summary]{.underline}**\n\nA Phase I Environmental Site Assessment (\\\"ESA\\\") was conducted by Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC (lead consultant Dr. James Whitfield, P.E.), Greensboro, North Carolina, dated October 28, 2024. The Phase I ESA was conducted in accordance with ASTM Standard E1527-21 and identified one Recognized Environmental Condition (\\\"REC\\\"): residual petroleum contamination from a former underground storage tank (\\\"UST\\\") located on the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102. The UST was associated with historic industrial operations on the site.\n\nA Phase II Environmental Site Assessment was subsequently conducted in August 2024, which confirmed the presence of petroleum-related soil contamination in the vicinity of the former UST. Contamination levels were found to exceed NCDEQ Inactive Hazardous Sites Branch (\\\"IHSB\\\") residential soil standards but were below non-residential (commercial/industrial) standards. Remediation activities, including soil excavation and disposal of approximately 340 cubic yards of contaminated soil, were completed in September 2024.\n\nNCDEQ issued a No Further Action (\\\"NFA\\\") letter dated September 30, 2024, approving the site for commercial use subject to the following conditions: (1) implementation of a soil vapor mitigation system in the structured parking garage constructed over the affected area; and (2) restriction of the affected portion of the parcel (approximately 0.38 acres in the northeast corner) to non-residential use.\n\n**Risk Factor.** The Bank notes that the NFA letter restricts the 0.38-acre area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 to non-residential use. However, the current project plans position 14 residential units in the building above this area. This potential conflict between the NFA use restriction and the proposed residential use must be resolved prior to closing or, at the Bank\\'s discretion, as a condition precedent to advances for construction above the podium level in the affected area. This matter is discussed in detail in Section 9.5 (Risk Factors).\n\nAn environmental indemnity agreement will be required from both the Borrower and the Guarantors (Elena Vasquez-Torres and Reginald K. Osei) as a condition of closing, indemnifying the Bank against all environmental claims, liabilities, and remediation costs arising from the condition of the Property.\n\n**[Section 9: Risk Factors and Mitigants]{.underline}**\n\n**[9.1 Risk Factor: LTC Ratio Exceedance and Zero Cash Equity]{.underline}**\n\n**Risk.** The loan-to-cost ratio of 83.77% exceeds the Bank\\'s maximum permitted LTC of 80% as set forth in both the executed term sheet and the Bank\\'s Commercial Real Estate Lending Policy. The Borrower is contributing zero dollars (\\$0) in direct cash equity to the Project. The only equity in the capital stack is the previously acquired land, which has an as-is appraised value of \\$8,900,000 and was acquired for \\$8,200,000. A portion of the capital used to fund the land acquisition was sourced from Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, an affiliate of the mezzanine lender, Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC. The combined senior loan and mezzanine loan totals \\$56,700,000, which equals 100% of total project costs, resulting in zero true ...(truncated)", "tool_call_id": "call_01_rPLsPGSVNwzm7bI6Sp0M5495", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/credit-memorandum.docx\"}"}
