{"turn": 4, "role": "tool", "result": "240 | 4.  SPE Covenants and Non-Consolidation Opinion. (a) The Borrower's operating agreement shall be amended to include comprehensive SPE covenants and an independent manager provision requiring the affirmative consent of an independent manager (not affiliated with the Borrower, any member, the mezzanine lender, or Thomas R. Chen) for any voluntary filing for bankruptcy, reorganization, or insolvency protection. (b) Delivery of a non-consolidation opinion from Borrower's counsel (Calloway & Strauss PLLC), addressed to the Bank, in form and substance satisfactory to the Bank and its counsel, opining that a court would not substantively consolidate the Borrower with any of its members, affiliates, or related entities.\n241 | 5.  Satisfactory Title. Issuance of a lender's title insurance policy by Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. (escrow officer Patricia Langley) insuring the first-priority lien of the Bank's deed of trust on both parcels (0821-04-72-3341 and 0821-04-72-4102), in the amount of $47,500,000, free and clear of the unsatisfied deed of trust from Triangle Commercial Bank (to be satisfied at closing through delivery of a payoff letter and simultaneous recording of a satisfaction or release), and subject only to approved standard title exceptions as determined by the Bank and its counsel.\n242 | 6.  Appraisal Confirmation. Confirmation that the as-completed appraised value is not less than $72,800,000 and the as-stabilized appraised value is not less than $76,200,000, as reflected in the appraisal by Ridgeline Appraisal Services Inc. dated November 15, 2024.\n243 | 7.  Environmental. (a) Satisfactory Phase I ESA and Phase II results from Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC; (b) evidence of the NCDEQ No Further Action letter dated September 30, 2024; (c) evidence that the soil vapor mitigation system design is incorporated into the approved construction plans; (d) resolution of the residential use restriction on the approximately 0.38-acre area in the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 — either through modification of the NFA letter, redesign of the affected units, or NCDEQ confirmation that residential use above the grade-level restricted area is permitted with engineering controls. At the Bank's discretion, resolution of the use restriction may be deferred to a condition precedent to advances for construction in the affected area rather than a closing condition.\n244 | 8.  Construction Contract. Execution of the GMP contract with Graystone Construction Group Inc. with a guaranteed maximum price of $34,750,000, together with delivery of a collateral assignment of the construction contract in favor of the Bank.\n245 | 9.  Architect's Contract. Delivery of a collateral assignment of the architect's agreement with Meridian Design Collective PC in favor of the Bank.\n246 | 10.  Insurance. Evidence of all required insurance coverages as specified in Section 8.1, including builder's risk, commercial general liability, umbrella/excess liability, workers' compensation, and flood insurance (if applicable).\n247 | 11.  Personal Guaranties. Execution and delivery of completion guaranties, carry guaranties, and 25% partial repayment guaranties by each of Elena Vasquez-Torres and Reginald K. Osei, each in form and substance satisfactory to the Bank and its counsel.\n248 | 12.  Organizational Documents. Delivery of certified copies of the Borrower's articles of organization, operating agreement (as amended for SPE covenants and independent manager provision), certificates of good standing from the North Carolina Secretary of State (current within 30 days of closing), and resolutions or written consents of members authorizing the loan, the execution and delivery of the loan documents, and the granting of the security interests.\n249 | 13.  Governmental Approvals. Evidence that all required governmental approvals, permits, and zoning compliance are in place, including a zoning compliance letter from the City of Durham confirming that the proposed development is permitted as of right under the MU-D zoning district.\n250 | 14.  Brightleaf Historic District Association. Evidence of Brightleaf Historic District Association approval of the project plans and architectural design, or evidence that the CC&Rs (Book 6984, Page 1155) do not require such approval or that the approval requirement has been waived.\n251 | 15.  Legal Opinions. Delivery of legal opinions from Borrower's counsel (Calloway & Strauss PLLC) addressed to the Bank, including opinions as to enforceability of the loan documents, authority of the Borrower and its members, the non-consolidation opinion described in Condition 4(b) above, and such other opinions as the Bank and its counsel may reasonably require.\n252 | 16.  Origination Fee. Payment of the origination fee of $475,000 (1.00% of $47,500,000).\n253 | 17.  Borrower Net Worth Certificate. Delivery of a net worth certificate from the Borrower, certified by the Managing Member, demonstrating a minimum net worth of not less than $12,000,000.\n254 | 18.  Guarantor Financial Statements. Delivery of current personal financial statements from each guarantor, together with verification of combined guarantor liquidity of not less than $5,000,000.\n255 | 19.  Additional Deliverables. All other documents, instruments, certificates, and opinions customarily required for a construction-to-mini-permanent loan transaction of this type and as the Bank and its counsel may reasonably require.\n256 | 10.2 Conditions Precedent to Each Advance\n257 | In addition to the closing conditions, each advance (draw) under the construction loan is subject to satisfaction of the following conditions:\n258 | 1.  No Default. No event of default exists under the loan documents, and no condition exists that with the giving of notice or the passage of time, or both, would constitute an event of default.\n259 | 2.  Representations and Warranties. All representations and warranties contained in the loan documents remain true and correct in all material respects as of the date of the advance.\n260 | 3.  Construction Inspector Report. Delivery of a satisfactory report from the Bank's third-party construction inspector confirming achievement of the applicable construction milestone and the percentage of completion warranted in the draw request.\n261 | 4.  AIA Application for Payment. Delivery of a completed AIA Application and Certificate for Payment (Form G702) and Continuation Sheet (Form G703) certified by the architect of record and the general contractor.\n262 | 5.  Lien Waivers. Delivery of partial lien waivers (in statutory form under N.C.G.S. Chapter 44A) from the general contractor and all subcontractors and materialmen for work performed and materials furnished through the prior draw period.\n263 | 6.  Title Date-Down. Delivery of a title date-down endorsement from Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. confirming no intervening liens, encumbrances, or title defects since the date of the prior title endorsement (or the original title policy date, for the first advance after closing).\n264 | 7.  Interest Reserve Adequacy Test. The Borrower must demonstrate that the remaining interest reserve balance, together with any additional funds the Borrower commits to deposit, is sufficient to cover projected interest costs through the earlier of (a) the next anticipated draw date or (b) 90 days from the date of the advance request, based on current SOFR rates and the projected outstanding loan balance. If the test is not satisfied, the Borrower must deposit additional funds into the interest reserve sub-account before the requested advance will be funded.\n265 | 8.  Equity First. The equity escrow deposit (if required per Condition 10.1.2) must have been fully disbursed and applied to project costs before any loan advance (other than the initial closing advance, Draw 1) is made.\n266 | 9.  Insurance Compliance. Delivery of evidence of continued compliance with all insurance requirements specified in Section 8.1.\n267 | 10.  No Material Adverse Change. No material adverse change has occurred in the financial condition of the Borrower, any guarantor, or the Project since the date of closing or the most recent advance, as applicable.\n268 | 11.  Budget Reconciliation. Delivery of an updated sources and uses reconciliation demonstrating that the Project remains within budget. If cost overruns exist, the Borrower must fund the overruns from equity (not loan proceeds or mezzanine loan proceeds) before any further advances will be made.\n269 | 10.3 Ongoing Covenants and Reporting Requirements\n270 | The loan documentation will include the following ongoing covenants and reporting requirements, which shall remain in effect throughout the term of the loan (including any extension period):\n271 | •  Minimum Borrower Net Worth: $12,000,000, tested annually within 120 days of the Borrower's fiscal year end, with delivery of a certified net worth certificate.\n272 | •  Minimum Combined Guarantor Liquidity: $5,000,000, tested quarterly with delivery of guarantor financial statements.\n273 | •  Negative Pledge / No Additional Debt: The Borrower may not incur any additional indebtedness, create any additional liens or encumbrances on the Property or any interest therein, or pledge any assets, without the Bank's prior written consent.\n274 | •  Monthly Construction Reports: Delivery of monthly construction progress reports to the Bank, including schedule updates, budget reconciliations, change order logs, and third-party inspector certifications.\n275 | •  SPE Covenants: The Borrower shall at all times maintain its status as a single-purpose entity in compliance with all SPE covenants set forth in the loan agreement, including maintenance of separate books and records, no commingling of assets or funds, arm's-length transactions with affiliates, independent manager provision, and all other separateness and identity requirements.\n276 | •  No Material Modifications: No material modification to the approved construction plans, specifications, or the GMP contract without the Bank's prior written consent. Any modification requiring Brightleaf Historic District Association approval must obtain such approval before the Bank's consent is given.\n277 | •  Governmental Compliance: The Borrower shall maintain all governmental permits, licenses, and approvals required for the construction and operation of the Project.\n278 | •  Environmental Compliance: The Borrower shall comply with all applicable environmental laws and regulations and with all conditions of the NFA letter dated September 30, 2024, including the installation and ongoing maintenance of the soil vapor mitigation system in the structured parking garage.\n279 | •  Transfer Restrictions: No transfer, assignment, or encumbrance of any membership interest in the Borrower without the Bank's prior written consent, subject to the intercreditor agreement provisions regarding the mezzanine lender's subordinated pledge.\n280 | •  Quarterly Guarantor Reporting: Delivery of quarterly personal financial statements from each guarantor.\n281 | •  Prompt Notice: The Borrower shall provide prompt written notice to the Bank of any litigation, environmental claim, governmental enforcement action, casualty, condemnation proceeding, or other material adverse development affecting the Borrower, any guarantor, or the Project.\n283 | Section 11: Extension Option Analysis\n284 | The executed term sheet provides for one 6-month extension of the mini-perm period, extending the loan maturity from August 28, 2028 to February 28, 2029. The term sheet states that the extension is \"subject to conditions\" without specifying the particular conditions. The Senior Loan Committee recommends that the following extension conditions be incorporated into the loan documentation:\n285 | (a) No Default. No event of default exists and no condition exists that with notice or lapse of time would constitute an event of default under the loan documents or the intercreditor agreement.\n286 | (b) Completion. Substantial completion of the Project has been achieved, and a final certificate of occupancy has been issued by the City of Durham for all units and commercial spaces in the Project.\n287 | (c) Minimum Occupancy. Not less than 75% of the residential units in the Project are leased to tenants under executed leases with terms of at least 12 months and are physically occupied as of the date of the extension request.\n288 | (d) Minimum DSCR. The debt service coverage ratio, calculated based on actual trailing three-month annualized in-place net operating income divided by the annual debt service on the outstanding loan balance at the then-current interest rate, is not less than 1.15x. This threshold is intentionally lower than the 1.25x underwriting standard to account for the expectation that the property may still be in lease-up at the time of the extension.\n289 | (e) Extension Fee. Payment of an extension fee equal to 0.25% of the then-outstanding principal balance of the loan.\n290 | (f) Interest Rate Cap. Delivery of evidence of an interest rate cap agreement for the extension period, with a cap strike rate approved by the Bank in its reasonable discretion, from a counterparty with a minimum long-term credit rating of A- (S&P) or A3 (Moody's).\n291 | (g) Intercreditor Agreement. The intercreditor agreement between the Bank and Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC remains in full force and effect, and no default exists thereunder.\n292 | (h) Updated Appraisal. Delivery of an updated appraisal (from an MAI-certified appraiser acceptable to the Bank) confirming that the LTV ratio based on the outstanding loan balance and the as-stabilized appraised value does not exceed 70%.\n293 | (i) Representations and Warranties. All representations and warranties contained in the loan documents remain true and correct in all material respects.\n294 | (j) Notice. Written notice of the Borrower's election to exercise the extension option, delivered to the Bank not less than 60 days prior to the mini-perm maturity date of August 28, 2028.\n295 | These extension conditions are designed to ensure that the Bank's credit position remains satisfactory at the time of the extension and that the Project is on a trajectory toward stabilization and permanent take-out financing.\n297 | Section 12: Regulatory and Compliance Considerations\n298 | Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. is a nationally chartered savings bank regulated by the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (\"OCC\"). As of the Bank's most recent Call Report, total consolidated assets are approximately $18,600,000,000. The proposed loan of $47,500,000 represents approximately 0.26% of total bank assets.\n299 | Concentration Risk. The Bank monitors its commercial real estate lending concentration relative to Tier 1 capital plus the allowance for loan and lease losses (\"ALLL\"), consistent with the interagency guidance on concentrations of credit risk issued by the OCC, Federal Reserve, and FDIC. The Bank's current CRE concentration levels remain within its board-approved concentration limits, and this loan does not cause any concentration threshold to be exceeded. The Bank's CRE portfolio management team has confirmed that the loan falls within the Bank's approved risk appetite and concentration parameters.\n300 | Lending Policy Compliance. The proposed loan is consistent with the Bank's Commercial Real Estate Lending Policy with respect to maximum LTV (70% for construction loans — the as-completed LTV of 65.25% is within this limit), minimum DSCR (1.25x stabilized — the projected DSCR of 1.31x is within this limit), and maximum LTC (80% — the LTC of 83.77% exceeds this limit but will be brought into compliance through the equity escrow condition).\n301 | FIRREA Appraisal Requirements. The appraisal by Linda M. Haverford, MAI, of Ridgeline Appraisal Services Inc. meets the requirements of FIRREA and OCC appraisal regulations. The appraiser is MAI-certified, is independent of the transaction parties, and the appraisal is dated November 15, 2024, which is within 12 months of the anticipated closing date.\n302 | Flood Determination. A flood zone determination will be obtained prior to closing to determine whether any portion of the Property is located within a Special Flood Hazard Area as designated by FEMA. If any portion of the Property is in a flood zone, the Bank will require flood insurance in the amount required by the National Flood Insurance Act and the Bank's policy.\n303 | Bank Secrecy Act / Anti-Money Laundering. The Bank has completed Know Your Customer (\"KYC\") and Customer Due Diligence (\"CDD\") procedures on the Borrower, all members (Elena Vasquez-Torres, Brightleaf Investors Group LLC, Thomas R. Chen, and Reginald K. Osei), and all guarantors. No adverse findings, matches to OFAC lists, or suspicious activity indicators were identified during the KYC/CDD review.\n304 | Fair Lending. This is a commercial purpose loan to a limited liability company for the construction of a mixed-use development. Fair lending regulations applicable to consumer and residential mortgage lending are not applicable to this transaction.\n305 | HVCRE Classification. This loan likely qualifies as a High Volatility Commercial Real Estate (\"HVCRE\") exposure under the Basel III capital rules (12 C.F.R. Part 3, Subpart D for national banks). HVCRE classification applies to acquisition, development, and construction loans where the borrower's contributed capital is less than 15% of the real estate's appraised as-completed value. The Borrower's contributed capital, including the proposed equity escrow of $2,140,000 and the land at acquisition cost of $8,200,000, totals $10,340,000. As a percentage of the as-completed appraised value of $72,800,000, the contributed capital equals $10,340,000 ÷ $72,800,000 = 14.2%, which is below the 15% threshold. To avoid HVCRE classification, total contributed capital would need to be at least $72,800,000 × 15% = $10,920,000, requiring an additional $580,000 beyond the proposed equity escrow of $2,140,000. The Senior Loan Committee accepts the HVCRE classification for this loan and acknowledges the resulting 150% risk weight for regulatory capital purposes.\n307 | Section 13: Third-Party Reports and Due Diligence Summary\n308 | The Bank has obtained or reviewed the following third-party reports and due diligence materials in connection with its underwriting of this loan:\n310 | All pending items must be completed and delivered to the Bank's satisfaction prior to closing.\n312 | Section 14: Recommendation and Approval\n313 | 14.1 Recommendation\n314 | Based on the foregoing analysis, the Commercial Real Estate Lending group recommends APPROVAL of the $47,500,000 construction-to-mini-permanent loan to Harborview Capital Partners LLC for The Piedmont at Brightleaf project, subject to all conditions set forth in Section 10 and resolution of the risk factors identified in Section 9.\n315 | The following conditions are designated as critical and must be fully satisfied prior to closing:\n316 | 1.  Equity Escrow. Deposit of $2,140,000 in verified cash equity into a Bank-controlled escrow account to bring the effective LTC into compliance with the Bank's 80% maximum (Sections 5.3, 9.1, 10.1.2).\n317 | 2.  Intercreditor Agreement. Full execution and delivery of the intercreditor agreement with Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC resolving the conflicting membership interest pledges and providing for subordination, standstill, cure rights, and bankruptcy protections (Sections 4.4, 9.4, 10.1.3).\n318 | 3.  SPE Covenants and Non-Consolidation Opinion. Amendment of the Borrower's operating agreement to include SPE covenants and an independent manager provision, and delivery of a non-consolidation opinion from Borrower's counsel (Sections 9.3, 10.1.4).\n319 | 4.  Interest Reserve Replenishment Covenant. Inclusion of mandatory borrower replenishment obligations and adequacy testing at each draw (Sections 6.2, 9.2, 10.2.7).\n320 | 5.  Environmental Resolution. Resolution of the NFA use restriction conflict regarding the 14 residential units above the restricted area (Sections 8.2, 9.5, 10.1.7).\n321 | 6.  Title Clearance. Satisfaction of the prior Triangle Commercial Bank deed of trust and issuance of satisfactory lender's title insurance (Sections 9.7, 10.1.5).\n322 | The loan presents acceptable credit risk given the strong project fundamentals, favorable LTV ratios (65.25% as-completed, 62.34% as-stabilized), the experienced development sponsor, and the adequate combined guarantor financial support ($41,300,000 net worth, $9,300,000 liquidity). The identified structural issues — including the LTC exceedance, zero borrower cash equity, interest reserve adequacy, and affiliate/SPE concerns — are addressable through the enumerated conditions of approval and appropriate loan documentation provisions. The Commercial Real Estate Lending group is confident that these conditions can be satisfied prior to the target closing date of February 28, 2025.\n323 | 14.2 Approval Signatures\n324 | SENIOR LOAN COMMITTEE — APPROVAL Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. Loan Number: CRE-2025-0342 Date: January 22, 2025\n325 | PREPARED BY / RECOMMENDED:\n326 | By: ________\n327 | Name: David Forsythe\n328 | Title: Senior Vice President, Commercial Real Estate Lending\n329 | Date: ________\n330 | REVIEWED AND CONCURRED:\n331 | By: ________\n332 | Name: ________\n333 | Title: Senior Credit Officer\n334 | Date: ________\n335 | APPROVED:\n336 | By: ________\n337 | Name: ________\n338 | Title: Chair, Senior Loan Committee\n339 | Date: ________\n340 | APPROVED BY SENIOR LOAN COMMITTEE — January 22, 2025\n341 | Committee Comments / Additional Conditions:\n342 | ________________________________________\n343 | ________________________________________\n344 | ________________________________________\n346 | Appendix A: Summary of Key Terms (One-Page Summary)\n349 | Appendix B: Organizational Chart (Diagram Description)\n350 | The following describes the organizational and capital structure of the Borrower and the Project:\n351 | ``` ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │                     THOMAS R. CHEN                               │ │                   (Common Control)                               │ │            ┌──────────┴──────────┐                               │ │            ▼                     ▼                               │ │  Brightleaf Investors     Brightleaf Mezzanine                   │ │  Group LLC                Capital LLC                            │ │  (NC SOS #2045612)        (NC SOS #2103988)                      │ │  28% Member               $9,200,000 Mezz Loan                  │ │            │               (12.50% fixed)                        │ │            │               Secured by: 100%                      │ │            │               membership interests                  │ │            │               (2nd priority per                     │ │            │               intercreditor)                        │ │            ▼                     │                               │ │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐             │ │  │     HARBORVIEW CAPITAL PARTNERS LLC              │◄───────────┘ │  │     (Borrower — NC LLC, SOS #1923847)            │ │  │     400 W. Main St., Suite 310, Durham, NC       │ │  │                                                   │ │  │  Members:                                         │ │  │  • Elena Vasquez-Torres — 62% (Managing Member)   │ │  │  • Brightleaf Investors Group LLC — 28%           │ │  │  • Reginald K. Osei — 10%                        │ │  └─────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┘ │                    │ Owns │                    ▼ │  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │  │   THE PIEDMONT AT BRIGHTLEAF                     │ │  │   601 & 615 Foster Street, Durham, NC 27701      │ │  │   212 Units / 18,400 SF Retail / 276 Parking     │ │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │                    ▲ │                    │ Senior Loan: $47,500,000 │                    │ Secured by: 1st priority deed of trust + │                    │ 1st priority membership interest pledge │                    │ (per intercreditor) │  ┌─────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┐ │  │   PINNACLE SAVINGS BANK, N.A.                    │ │  │   (Senior Lender)                                │ │  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n352 | GUARANTORS (Completion, Carry, 25% Partial Repayment): • Elena Vasquez-Torres (Net Worth: $29,800,000) • Reginald K. Osei (Net Worth: $11,500,000) ```\n354 | Appendix C: Sources and Uses Table (Full Detail)\n355 | Sources of Funds\n357 | Uses of Funds\n359 | Reconciliation: Total Sources ($56,700,000) = Total Uses ($56,700,000).\n360 | END OF CREDIT MEMORANDUM — CRE-2025-0342 — Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. — CONFIDENTIAL\n", "tool_call_id": "call_00_ET_4xPTAqCfSlHvnGZY1RWI1487", "tool_name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd $WORKSPACE_DIR && python3 -c \\\"\\nimport docx\\nd = docx.Document('documents/credit-memorandum.docx')\\nfor i,p in enumerate(d.paragraphs[240:], start=240):\\n    if p.text.strip():\\n        print(i, '|', p.text)\\n\\\"\"}"}
