{"turn": 6, "role": "tool", "result": "**EXECUTIVE SUMMARY --- SELF-CONTAINED APPRAISAL REPORT**\n\n**The Piedmont at Brightleaf**\n\n**Proposed 212-Unit Class A Mixed-Use Residential and Retail Development**\n\n601 and 615 Foster Street Durham, Durham County, North Carolina 27701\n\n**Prepared For:**\n\nPinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. 215 South Tryon Street, 18th Floor Charlotte, North Carolina 28202\n\nAttention: David Forsythe, SVP, Commercial Real Estate Lending\n\nBank Internal Loan Number: CRE-2025-0342\n\n**Prepared By:**\n\nRidgeline Appraisal Services Inc. Raleigh, North Carolina\n\n**Appraiser of Record:** Linda M. Haverford, MAI NC State Certified General Real Estate Appraiser License No. A-8291\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Date of Value:**                  **November 15, 2024**\n  ----------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  **Date of Report:**                 November 15, 2024\n\n  **File Reference:**                 RAS-2024-1147\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThis executive summary is excerpted from the full self-contained appraisal report. The complete report comprises approximately 187 pages and is incorporated herein by reference. This summary is intended for convenience of reference only and should be read in conjunction with the full report.\n\nCONFIDENTIAL --- PREPARED EXCLUSIVELY FOR THE USE OF PINNACLE SAVINGS BANK, N.A.\n\n**[Section 1 --- Transmittal Letter]{.underline}**\n\nNovember 15, 2024\n\nMr. David Forsythe Senior Vice President, Commercial Real Estate Lending Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. 215 South Tryon Street, 18th Floor Charlotte, North Carolina 28202\n\nRe: Executive Summary of Self-Contained Appraisal Report --- The Piedmont at Brightleaf, 601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, Durham County, North Carolina 27701 Ridgeline File Reference: RAS-2024-1147 Bank Loan Number: CRE-2025-0342\n\nDear Mr. Forsythe:\n\nAt the request of Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. (the \\\"Client\\\"), Ridgeline Appraisal Services Inc. has prepared a self-contained appraisal report of the above-referenced property. The purpose of the appraisal is to estimate (i) the \\\"As-Is\\\" market value of the land in its current condition, (ii) the \\\"As-Completed\\\" market value of the proposed development upon completion of construction, and (iii) the \\\"As-Stabilized\\\" market value of the proposed development upon achieving stabilized occupancy. The intended use of this appraisal is to assist the Client in evaluating a proposed construction loan for the subject property. The intended user of this appraisal is Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A., and its successors and assigns. No other use or user is intended or authorized.\n\nThe appraisal was prepared in conformance with the Uniform Standards of Professional Appraisal Practice (\\\"USPAP\\\"), as promulgated by The Appraisal Foundation, and in compliance with the requirements of Title XI of the Financial Institutions Reform, Recovery, and Enforcement Act of 1989 (\\\"FIRREA\\\"), as amended. The appraisal also conforms to the appraisal guidelines and requirements of Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A., as communicated to the appraiser in the engagement letter dated October 7, 2024.\n\nBased upon the analysis, opinions, and conclusions set forth in the full appraisal report, the value conclusions are summarized as follows:\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Valuation Scenario**                           **Effective Date**                **Concluded Market Value**\n  ------------------------------------------------ --------------------------------- ----------------------------\n  As-Is (Land Value)                               November 15, 2024                 **\\$8,900,000**\n\n  As-Completed (Upon Completion of Construction)   Prospective --- August 31, 2027   **\\$72,800,000**\n\n  As-Stabilized (At 95% Occupancy)                 Prospective --- May 2028          **\\$76,200,000**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe effective date of valuation for the as-is value is November 15, 2024. The prospective valuation dates are based on the developer\\'s projected construction timeline and the appraiser\\'s absorption analysis.\n\nThe full appraisal report comprises approximately 187 pages, including all supporting data, analyses, addenda, and certifications. This executive summary is excerpted therefrom for convenience of reference and should be read in conjunction with the complete report. All extraordinary assumptions, hypothetical conditions, and limiting conditions set forth in the full report apply equally to this executive summary.\n\nRespectfully submitted,\n\nRIDGELINE APPRAISAL SERVICES INC.\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nLinda M. Haverford, MAI NC State Certified General Real Estate Appraiser License No. A-8291\n\nDate: November 15, 2024\n\n**[Section 2 --- Subject Property Identification]{.underline}**\n\n**Property Name:** The Piedmont at Brightleaf\n\n**Street Address:** 601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, Durham County, North Carolina 27701\n\n**Parcel Identification Numbers:** Parcel 0821-04-72-3341 (601 Foster Street) and Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 (615 Foster Street). Both parcels are recorded in the Office of the Durham County Register of Deeds and are reflected on the Durham County tax maps.\n\n**Total Land Area:** The subject property consists of two contiguous parcels totaling approximately 3.47 acres, or approximately 151,153 square feet. The parcels are being developed as a single integrated site under unified ownership.\n\n**Shape and Topography:** The combined site is irregular in shape, with approximately 420 feet of frontage along Foster Street on the western boundary. The topography is generally level with a slight grade change of approximately four feet from the northern boundary to the southern boundary, sloping gently to the south. The grade differential is not considered significant and is accommodated within the proposed site plan and building design without extraordinary foundation or grading costs.\n\n**Current Condition:** The site is vacant improved land. Former tobacco warehouse structures that previously occupied both parcels were demolished in August 2024 pursuant to demolition permits issued by the City of Durham. Environmental remediation activities associated with a former underground storage tank on Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 were completed in September 2024, as further described in Section 8 of this report. As of the date of inspection, the site has been cleared of all structures and debris and has been rough-graded in preparation for construction.\n\n**Frontage and Access:** The property has approximately 420 feet of frontage along Foster Street, a two-lane paved public roadway with on-street parking and sidewalks. Secondary vehicular access is available from a 20-foot public alley along the eastern boundary of the site. The proposed site plan incorporates a primary vehicular entrance from Foster Street and a secondary entrance and loading/service access from the public alley to the east.\n\n**Utilities:** All municipal utilities are available to the site, including municipal water and sewer service provided by the City of Durham Department of Water Management, electric service provided by Duke Energy Carolinas, natural gas service provided by Dominion Energy North Carolina, and telecommunications and fiber-optic service from multiple providers.\n\n**Flood Zone:** The subject property is located in Flood Zone X (area of minimal flood hazard) as depicted on the Federal Emergency Management Agency Flood Insurance Rate Map (\\\"FIRM\\\"), Community Panel Number 3710406400J, with an effective date of May 2, 2019. No portion of the site lies within a Special Flood Hazard Area, and flood insurance is not required by federal regulation.\n\n**Zoning:** The property is zoned MU-D (Mixed Use --- Downtown) under the City of Durham Unified Development Ordinance. The MU-D zoning classification permits residential, retail, office, and mixed-use structures by right, with a maximum building height of 120 feet. The proposed development, at six stories and approximately 78 feet in height, is well within the applicable height limitation. Minimum parking requirements under the MU-D district have been satisfied by the proposed structured parking garage providing 276 spaces. No variances, special use permits, or conditional use approvals are required for the proposed development as currently designed.\n\n**Tax Assessment:** The current combined assessed value of the two parcels, as reflected on the Durham County tax rolls following the post-demolition reassessment, is \\$7,250,000 (land value only). The Durham County Tax Identification Numbers correspond to the parcel identification numbers referenced above. Property taxes for the 2024 tax year are current and paid.\n\n**Ownership:** The subject property is owned by Harborview Capital Partners LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company, organized and existing under the laws of the State of North Carolina, NC Secretary of State File Number 1923847, with principal offices at 400 West Main Street, Suite 310, Durham, North Carolina 27701. The managing member of Harborview Capital Partners LLC is Elena Vasquez-Torres.\n\n**Recorded Encumbrances: The appraiser reviewed the preliminary title commitment prepared by Aldersgate Title & Escrow Inc. (Commitment Number NC-2024-88712, dated December 5, 2024). Although the title commitment is dated subsequent to the effective date of the appraisal, it was reviewed for completeness and to confirm the status of encumbrances of record. The following encumbrances were noted:**\n\n> \\(a\\) A 20-foot utility easement along the western boundary of the combined site, benefiting Duke Energy Carolinas, for the purpose of installing, maintaining, and repairing overhead and underground utility lines and related infrastructure;\n>\n> \\(b\\) A stormwater management easement in favor of the City of Durham, recorded in Book 7421, Page 212, Durham County Register of Deeds, affecting approximately 0.22 acres in the southern portion of the site and requiring maintenance of stormwater detention and conveyance facilities;\n>\n> \\(c\\) A Declaration of Covenants, Conditions, and Restrictions (\\\"CC&Rs\\\") recorded in Book 6984, Page 1155, Durham County Register of Deeds, imposed by the Brightleaf Historic District Association, Inc., establishing architectural review and design standards applicable to all new construction and major exterior modifications within the Brightleaf Historic District; and\n>\n> \\(d\\) A deed of trust in favor of Triangle Commercial Bank, recorded in 2018, with an original principal amount of \\$3,400,000, encumbering both parcels.\n\nThe appraiser\\'s analysis assumes that the encumbrances described in items (a), (b), and (c) above do not materially affect the development potential or value of the property. The proposed site plan accommodates the utility easement and stormwater management easement, and the architectural design has been reviewed and approved by the Brightleaf Historic District Association in accordance with the CC&Rs. With respect to item (d), the appraiser assumes that the existing deed of trust in favor of Triangle Commercial Bank will be satisfied and released prior to or concurrently with the closing of the proposed construction financing.\n\n**[Section 3 --- Project Description]{.underline}**\n\n**Developer:** Harborview Capital Partners LLC, a North Carolina limited liability company, with Elena Vasquez-Torres serving as Managing Member. The developer has a demonstrated track record in the Triangle market, having completed four prior mixed-use residential and commercial development projects in the Raleigh-Durham area totaling approximately \\$128 million in aggregate development costs. Prior projects include The Edison at West Village (84 units, Durham, completed 2019), The Meridian on Glenwood (116 units, Raleigh, completed 2020), Harborview Lofts (64 units, Chapel Hill, completed 2021), and The Apex at Chatham (92 units, Cary, completed 2023).\n\n**Architect:** Meridian Design Collective PC, a North Carolina professional corporation with offices in Durham, North Carolina. The firm specializes in urban infill, mixed-use, and multifamily residential design, with particular expertise in the historic districts of downtown Durham.\n\n**General Contractor:** Graystone Construction Group Inc., a North Carolina corporation, NC General Contractor License Number 78442, with principal offices at 1200 Fayetteville Road, Raleigh, North Carolina 27603. The president of the firm is Mark D. Sullivan. The construction contract is a guaranteed maximum price (\\\"GMP\\\") contract with a total GMP of \\$34,750,000. The GMP contract has been executed and a copy was provided to the appraiser for review.\n\n**Project Type:** The proposed project is a ground-up construction of a Class A mixed-use residential and retail development to be known as \\\"The Piedmont at Brightleaf.\\\"\n\n**Total Gross Building Area:** Approximately 287,500 square feet.\n\n**Stories:** The building will consist of six stories --- five residential floors above a one-story podium level containing ground-floor retail space and a four-level structured parking garage.\n\n**Residential Unit Mix:** The project will contain a total of 212 residential rental units distributed as follows:\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Unit Type**     **Number of Units**   **Average Unit Size (SF)**   **Total Residential SF**\n  ----------------- --------------------- ---------------------------- --------------------------\n  One-Bedroom       142                   725                          102,950\n\n  Two-Bedroom       56                    1,050                        58,800\n\n  Three-Bedroom     14                    1,350                        18,900\n\n  **Total**         **212**               **--- ---**                  **180,650**\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Retail Space:** The ground-floor podium level will include approximately 18,400 square feet of retail space configured across seven individual retail bays. The bays range in size from approximately 1,800 square feet to 3,600 square feet and are designed for neighborhood-serving commercial uses, including restaurants, cafes, personal services, and boutique retail. The retail spaces will feature floor-to-ceiling glass storefronts, direct street access from Foster Street, and independent HVAC and utility metering.\n\n**Parking:** The project includes a four-level structured parking garage integrated into the podium level, providing a total of 276 parking spaces, representing a ratio of approximately 1.30 spaces per residential unit. The parking allocation includes 18 dedicated retail parking spaces, 14 ADA-accessible spaces, and 22 electric vehicle charging stations equipped with Level 2 charging infrastructure. Access to the parking garage will be controlled by a card-access system with separate entrances for residential and retail/visitor parking.\n\n**Amenities:** The project will feature a comprehensive amenity package consistent with Class A apartment communities in the downtown Durham market, including:\n\n> • Rooftop terrace with outdoor kitchen, fire pit, and lounge seating areas;\n>\n> • Fitness center (approximately 2,800 square feet) with cardio, strength, and yoga studio areas;\n>\n> • Co-working lounge (approximately 1,500 square feet) with private meeting rooms and high-speed internet;\n>\n> • Resort-style pool deck with sundeck and cabanas;\n>\n> • Dog park with pet wash station;\n>\n> • Secured package room with automated locker system;\n>\n> • Bicycle storage room with capacity for 48 bicycles and a bicycle repair station;\n>\n> • Controlled-access building entry with intercom system and 24-hour video surveillance.\n\n**Building Construction:** The building will be Type III-A construction, consisting of a wood-frame superstructure over a reinforced concrete podium. The foundation system will consist of a reinforced concrete mat foundation supported on driven piles, based on geotechnical recommendations provided by Carolina Geotechnical Associates. Exterior materials will include a combination of brick veneer, fiber cement panels, and aluminum curtain wall glazing systems, designed to be consistent with the Brightleaf Historic District architectural standards and the requirements of the CC&Rs. Interior unit finishes will be Class A quality, including quartz countertops, stainless steel appliances, luxury vinyl plank flooring, and in-unit washer and dryer connections.\n\n**Projected Construction Timeline:** Construction is projected to commence upon closing of the construction loan and is estimated to require approximately 30 months from commencement to substantial completion. Projected substantial completion is August 31, 2027.\n\n**Total Project Costs:** The total estimated project cost is \\$56,700,000, as set forth in the developer\\'s detailed development budget reviewed by the appraiser. The budget is summarized as follows:\n\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Cost Category**                                              **Amount**\n  -------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Land Acquisition                                               \\$8,200,000\n\n  Hard Construction Costs (GMP)                                  \\$34,750,000\n\n  Soft Costs (architecture, engineering, permits, legal, etc.)   \\$4,150,000\n\n  Interest Reserve                                               \\$4,850,000\n\n  Origination Fee                                                \\$475,000\n\n  Developer Fee                                                  \\$2,835,000\n\n  Contingency                                                    \\$1,440,000\n\n  **Total Project Costs**                                        **\\$56,700,000**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Financing:** The developer has obtained a commitment for a \\$47,500,000 senior construction loan from Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A. (the subject of this appraisal engagement) and a \\$9,200,000 mezzanine loan from Brightleaf Mezzanine Capital LLC. The developer\\'s equity contribution, inclusive of the value of the land, is projected to comprise the balance of the capital stack.\n\n**[Section 4 --- Market Analysis Summary]{.underline}**\n\n**[Durham Multifamily Market Overview]{.underline}**\n\nDurham, North Carolina, has experienced robust population and employment growth over the past decade, driven principally by the expansion of the Research Triangle\\'s technology, healthcare, biotechnology, and life sciences sectors. The Durham-Chapel Hill Metropolitan Statistical Area (\\\"MSA\\\") population has grown at an annualized rate of approximately 2.1% over the past five years, outpacing both the national average and the average for the state of North Carolina. The city\\'s economic base is anchored by major employers including Duke University, the Duke University Health System, IBM, Hartleigh Investments, and numerous biotechnology and pharmaceutical companies clustered in and around Research Triangle Park.\n\nDowntown Durham, in particular, has undergone significant revitalization over the past fifteen years, with substantial public and private investment transforming the American Tobacco, Brightleaf, and West Village districts into vibrant mixed-use neighborhoods. The redevelopment of historic tobacco warehouses and surrounding properties has attracted a critical mass of residential, retail, restaurant, and entertainment uses that have established downtown Durham as one of the most desirable urban neighborhoods in the Research Triangle region.\n\n**[Competitive Submarket]{.underline}**\n\nThe subject property is located within the downtown Durham / Brightleaf submarket. As of the third quarter of 2024, the submarket contained approximately 4,850 Class A multifamily rental units across 14 properties. Overall Class A vacancy in the submarket was approximately 5.8%, representing a slight increase from the cyclical low of 4.3% observed in the first quarter of 2023 but still indicative of healthy market fundamentals. The average asking rent for Class A units in the submarket was approximately \\$2,185 per month, reflecting an annualized rent growth rate of approximately 3.4% over the trailing twelve-month period. New supply has been moderate, with approximately 680 units delivered in 2023 and an estimated 520 units projected for delivery in 2024.\n\n**[Comparable Rental Properties]{.underline}**\n\nThe appraiser identified and analyzed the following four properties as primary rental comparables for the subject:\n\n**Comparable 1: The Bellweather at American Tobacco** --- Located at 318 Blackwell Street, approximately 0.4 miles south of the subject. This 186-unit Class A apartment community was completed in 2021 and features a unit mix of studios, one-bedroom, and two-bedroom units with a full amenity package including a rooftop pool and fitness center. As of the date of analysis, the property reported an average asking rent of \\$2,310 per month and an occupancy rate of 96.2%.\n\n**Comparable 2: Warehouse Lofts on Rigsbee** --- Located at 205 North Rigsbee Avenue, approximately 0.6 miles west of the subject. This 142-unit Class A adaptive reuse project was completed in 2020, featuring loft-style units with exposed brick and heavy timber construction. Average asking rent was \\$2,175 per month with an occupancy rate of 94.8%. The slightly lower rent and occupancy levels relative to other comparables are attributed to the property\\'s more limited amenity package and older vintage relative to the newest deliveries.\n\n**Comparable 3: District on Main** --- Located at 411 West Main Street, approximately 0.3 miles southwest of the subject. This 224-unit Class A apartment community was completed in 2022 and is situated in the heart of the West Village district. The property features ground-floor retail, structured parking, and an extensive amenity package. Average asking rent was \\$2,245 per month with an occupancy rate of 95.5%.\n\n**Comparable 4: Foster Street Station** --- Located at 515 Foster Street, approximately 0.2 miles north of the subject. This 108-unit Class A development was completed in 2019 and represents the closest comparable to the subject in terms of immediate location. The property reported an average asking rent of \\$2,095 per month and an occupancy rate of 97.1%. The lower average rent reflects the property\\'s smaller unit sizes and the absence of premium amenities such as a rooftop terrace and pool.\n\n**[Projected Rent Levels for Subject]{.underline}**\n\nBased on the comparable rental analysis, the appraiser projects the following stabilized rent levels for the subject property:\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Unit Type**           **Projected Rent Range**   **Projected Average Rent**\n  ----------------------- -------------------------- ----------------------------\n  One-Bedroom             \\$2,100 -- \\$2,350/month   \\$2,175/month\n\n  Two-Bedroom             \\$2,450 -- \\$2,750/month   \\$2,550/month\n\n  Three-Bedroom           \\$2,950 -- \\$3,200/month   \\$3,050/month\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe weighted average monthly rent across all 212 units, incorporating the unit mix and projected rents, is approximately \\$2,245 per month. These projections reflect the subject\\'s advantages as a newly constructed Class A product with a superior amenity package and prime Brightleaf district location.\n\n**[Retail Market]{.underline}**\n\nThe downtown Durham retail market continues to benefit from the increasing residential density and foot traffic generated by the neighborhood\\'s multifamily and mixed-use developments. As of the third quarter of 2024, retail vacancy in the downtown core was approximately 4.2%, and triple-net rents for ground-floor retail space in comparable mixed-use projects ranged from \\$26.00 to \\$34.00 per square foot on a triple-net basis. The appraiser projects a stabilized retail rent of \\$30.00 per square foot NNN for the subject\\'s 18,400 square feet of ground-floor retail space, reflecting the property\\'s excellent visibility, street-level access, and location within the active Brightleaf district.\n\n**[Absorption Projection]{.underline}**\n\nBased on historical absorption rates observed at the comparable properties during their initial lease-up periods, the appraiser estimates that the subject will achieve stabilized occupancy (defined as 95%) within approximately eight to ten months following receipt of the certificate of occupancy. The projected absorption rate is approximately 22 to 25 units per month, consistent with the pace observed at The Bellweather at American Tobacco (24 units per month) and District on Main (23 units per month) during their respective lease-up periods.\n\n**[Demand Drivers]{.underline}**\n\nPrimary demand drivers for the subject property include Duke University and the Duke University Health System (the largest private employer in Durham), Research Triangle Park (located approximately 10 miles southeast), the downtown Durham employment corridor, and proximity to significant cultural and entertainment venues including the Durham Bulls Athletic Park, the Durham Performing Arts Center, and the American Tobacco Campus. Additionally, the growing presence of technology firms and startups in the downtown Durham area continues to attract a young, professionally employed renter demographic that represents the target market for the subject property.\n\n**[Section 5 --- Highest and Best Use Analysis]{.underline}**\n\n**[As Vacant]{.underline}**\n\nThe highest and best use of the subject property, as if vacant and available for development, was analyzed in accordance with the four standard tests: legal permissibility, physical possibility, financial feasibility, and maximum productivity. The site\\'s MU-D zoning permits a range of uses including residential, retail, office, hotel, and mixed-use development, all by right, with structures up to 120 feet in height. The site\\'s physical characteristics --- including its 3.47-acre size, generally level topography, availability of all municipal utilities, and favorable street frontage --- support a variety of development programs. Alternative uses considered in the analysis included office development, hotel development, and exclusively retail development. Each alternative was evaluated against a mixed-use residential and retail program. Based on the land residual analysis performed in the full appraisal report, a mixed-use residential and retail development of the type and scale proposed for the subject was determined to produce the highest land residual value and is therefore concluded to represent the highest and best use of the land as if vacant.\n\n**[As Proposed (Improved)]{.underline}**\n\nThe highest and best use of the property as proposed --- that is, assuming construction of the 212-unit mixed-use residential and retail development as described in Section 3 --- satisfies all four tests. The proposed development is legally permissible under MU-D zoning without the need for variances or special permits. It is physically possible given the site\\'s dimensions, topography, and infrastructure availability. It is financially feasible based on the income projections and cost analysis set forth in this report, which indicate that the prospective stabilized net operating income will support a market value in excess of the total development cost. Finally, the proposed development is maximally productive relative to alternative development programs analyzed by the appraiser, as it generates the highest return on cost among the programs considered.\n\n**[Conclusion]{.underline}**\n\nThe proposed development of The Piedmont at Brightleaf --- a 212-unit Class A mixed-use residential and retail project --- represents the highest and best use of the subject property, both as if vacant and as proposed.\n\n**[Section 6 --- Valuation Methodology and Conclusions]{.underline}**\n\n**[Section 6.1 --- As-Is Land Valuation]{.underline}**\n\n**Approach:** The as-is market value of the subject land was estimated using the sales comparison approach, which is the most reliable and widely accepted methodology for valuing vacant land in an active market with sufficient comparable data. The subject is valued in its current condition as of the effective date: vacant, cleared, and rough-graded, with environmental remediation completed and all municipal utilities available.\n\n**Comparable Land Sales:** The appraiser identified and analyzed four recent sales of comparable development land in the downtown Durham and adjacent urban submarkets. Each comparable was adjusted for differences in location, size, shape, access, utilities, topography, and market conditions (time of sale). The comparable sales and adjustments are summarized below:\n\n**Sale 1:** A 2.81-acre parcel located on West Chapel Hill Street, Durham, sold in July 2024 for \\$7,447,000, or \\$2,650,000 per acre. The parcel is zoned for mixed-use development and is located approximately 0.5 miles west of the subject. After adjustment for the subject\\'s superior street frontage and slightly less favorable vehicular access from a secondary roadway, the adjusted rate is \\$2,510,000 per acre.\n\n**Sale 2:** A 1.95-acre parcel located on Morris Street, Durham, sold in April 2024 for \\$5,460,000, or \\$2,800,000 per acre. The parcel is smaller than the subject and has a more regular shape, which typically commands a premium on a per-acre basis for efficient development. After adjustment for the subject\\'s larger size and irregular configuration, the adjusted rate is \\$2,620,000 per acre.\n\n**Sale 3:** A 4.12-acre parcel located on Mangum Street, Durham, sold in January 2024 for \\$9,888,000, or \\$2,400,000 per acre. The parcel is located in a transitional area approximately 0.7 miles north of the downtown core and was adjusted upward for the subject\\'s superior Brightleaf district location and proximity to established amenities. The adjusted rate is \\$2,550,000 per acre.\n\n**Sale 4:** A 3.05-acre assemblage of two parcels located on Corporation Street, Durham, sold in September 2023 for \\$7,168,000, or \\$2,350,000 per acre. The sale occurred approximately 14 months prior to the effective date and was adjusted upward for market conditions (positive price trend in downtown Durham land values) and for the subject\\'s superior frontage and access. The adjusted rate is \\$2,480,000 per acre.\n\n**Indicated Value Range:** The adjusted comparable sales indicate a range of \\$2,480,000 to \\$2,620,000 per acre for the subject land.\n\n**Selected Rate:** After reconciliation of the comparable data, placing greatest weight on Sales 1 and 3 as most similar in size and location to the subject, the appraiser selected a rate of approximately \\$2,565,000 per acre.\n\n**As-Is Land Value:** 3.47 acres × \\$2,565,000 per acre = \\$8,900,550, rounded to **\\$8,900,000**.\n\n**Reconciliation:** The concluded as-is land value of \\$8,900,000 is consistent with the land acquisition cost of \\$8,200,000, which was the price paid by Harborview Capital Partners LLC in early 2024 prior to the demolition of the existing warehouse structures and the completion of environmental remediation. The demolition and remediation activities, which cost approximately \\$680,000, have contributed to the current enhanced value of the land in its cleared and development-ready condition.\n\n**[Section 6.2 --- As-Completed Valuation]{.underline}**\n\n**Approaches Used:** The as-completed market value of the subject property was estimated using the income capitalization approach as the primary valuation methodology, supported by the sales comparison approach as a secondary check on reasonableness.\n\n**Income Capitalization Approach**\n\nThe \\\"as-completed\\\" value reflects the market value of the proposed development upon completion of construction but prior to the achievement of stabilized occupancy. This valuation scenario recognizes that, at the point of construction completion, the property will not yet have reached its projected stabilized occupancy of 95% and the owner will incur costs and experience income loss during the lease-up period. Accordingly, the as-completed value is derived by deducting from the as-stabilized value the estimated costs and economic impact of the lease-up period.\n\nThe prospective stabilized net operating income (\\\"NOI\\\") of the subject, as detailed in Section 7 below, is \\$4,876,800 per annum. Applied at the selected capitalization rate of 6.40%, the indicated as-stabilized value is \\$76,200,000 (see Section 6.3 below).\n\n**Lease-Up Deductions from As-Stabilized Value:**\n\nThe appraiser estimated the following deductions to account for the lease-up period between construction completion and stabilization:\n\n> • **Estimated lease-up period:** 9 months (midpoint of the 8- to 10-month range projected in Section 4), commencing upon receipt of the certificate of occupancy.\n>\n> • **Lost rental income during lease-up:** Assuming a linear absorption from 0% to 95% occupancy over the 9-month period, the effective average occupancy during lease-up is approximately 47.5%. The resulting lost rental income relative to stabilized occupancy (95%) is estimated at approximately \\$2,450,000.\n>\n> • **Lease-up costs:** Additional marketing expenditures, concessions to early tenants (estimated at an average of one month\\'s free rent per unit), and supplemental on-site leasing staff are estimated to total approximately \\$680,000.\n>\n> • **Total lease-up adjustment:** Approximately \\$3,130,000.\n>\n> • **Discount for lease-up risk and timing:** An additional deduction of approximately \\$270,000 is applied to account for the risk and present-value discount associated with the timing uncertainty of the lease-up period.\n>\n> • **Total deduction from as-stabilized value:** \\$3,130,000 + \\$270,000 = \\$3,400,000.\n\n**As-Completed Value via Income Approach:** \\$76,200,000 − \\$3,400,000 = **\\$72,800,000**.\n\n**Sales Comparison Approach (Supportive)**\n\nAs a supportive check on the income capitalization approach, the appraiser analyzed three comparable sales of recently constructed mixed-use multifamily properties in the Research Triangle area. The sales indicated a price range of \\$330,000 to \\$360,000 per residential unit, after adjustment for the retail component. Applied to the subject\\'s 212 residential units, the midpoint of approximately \\$343,000 per unit indicates a value of approximately \\$72,700,000, which is highly supportive of the income approach conclusion.\n\n**Concluded As-Completed Market Value: \\$72,800,000**\n\n**[Section 6.3 --- As-Stabilized Valuation]{.underline}**\n\n**Approach:** The as-stabilized market value was estimated using the income capitalization approach, specifically the direct capitalization method.\n\n**Stabilization Assumption:** Stabilized occupancy is defined as 95% across all residential units and retail bays, consistent with the observed stabilized occupancy levels at the comparable properties in the downtown Durham submarket.\n\n**Projected Stabilization Date:** The appraiser projects that the subject will achieve stabilized occupancy approximately nine months following projected substantial completion, or approximately May 2028.\n\n**Capitalization Rate Analysis:** The overall capitalization rate was derived from an analysis of five comparable property sales in the Raleigh-Durham metropolitan market, all involving Class A multifamily or mixed-use residential properties with recent construction dates. The observed capitalization rates for these transactions ranged from 5.75% to 7.10%, with a mean of approximately 6.35% and a median of approximately 6.40%. The variance in observed rates reflects differences in property quality, location, tenant profile, and the proportion of retail versus residential income.\n\n**Selected Capitalization Rate: 6.40%.** The selected rate is positioned at the median of the comparable range and reflects the subject\\'s status as a new-construction, Class A property in a prime downtown location with strong demand fundamentals. The rate also accounts for the current interest rate environment, which has stabilized following the tightening cycle of 2022-2023, with the 10-year U.S. Treasury yield trading at approximately 4.40% as of the effective date.\n\n**Stabilized Net Operating Income:** \\$4,876,800 per annum (as detailed in Section 7 below).\n\n**As-Stabilized Value Calculation:**\n\n\\$4,876,800 ÷ 0.0640 = **\\$76,200,000**\n\n**Cross-Check:** The indicated as-stabilized value of \\$76,200,000 implies a price per residential unit of approximately \\$359,434 (\\$76,200,000 ÷ 212 units). This metric falls within the range of \\$340,000 to \\$380,000 per unit observed for Class A new-construction multifamily properties in the downtown Durham submarket, confirming the reasonableness of the valuation conclusion. On a gross building area basis, the indicated value equates to approximately \\$265.04 per square foot (\\$76,200,000 ÷ 287,500 square feet), which is consistent with comparable market data.\n\n**[Section 7 --- Projected Stabilized Income and Expense Analysis]{.underline}**\n\nThe following income and expense projections are based on the comparable rental data and operating expense analysis set forth in the full appraisal report. All projections assume stabilized occupancy at 95% and are presented on an annual basis.\n\n**[Gross Potential Rental Income --- Residential]{.underline}**\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Unit Type**          **Units**      **Average Monthly Rent**   **Annual Rent per Unit**   **Annual Gross Potential Rent**\n  ---------------------- -------------- -------------------------- -------------------------- ---------------------------------\n  One-Bedroom            142            \\$2,175                    \\$26,100                   \\$3,706,200\n\n  Two-Bedroom            56             \\$2,550                    \\$30,600                   \\$1,713,600\n\n  Three-Bedroom          14             \\$3,050                    \\$36,600                   \\$512,400\n\n  **Total / Subtotal**   **212**        ---                        ---                        **\\$5,932,200**\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe gross potential residential rent at face rents totals \\$5,932,200, reflecting a weighted average monthly rent of approximately \\$2,333 per unit. However, the appraiser has also performed a blended analysis incorporating market concessions (estimated at approximately one-half month per unit on average at stabilization, reflecting the competitive environment for newly delivered units), rent premiums for specific unit features (including floor-level premiums for upper-floor units, view premiums for units with skyline or courtyard views, and upgraded finish package premiums), and adjustments for unit layout and orientation. This blended analysis produces a normalized annual residential income of **\\$5,712,000**, which equates to a blended effective average rent of approximately \\$2,245 per month across all 212 units. The normalized figure is used in the income analysis below.\n\n**[Gross Potential Rental Income --- Retail]{.underline}**\n\nThe subject\\'s 18,400 square feet of ground-floor retail space is projected to generate gross potential rental income of **\\$552,000** per annum, based on the concluded market rent of \\$30.00 per square foot on a triple-net basis (18,400 SF × \\$30.00/SF NNN = \\$552,000). Under the triple-net retail lease structure, tenants are responsible for their pro rata share of property taxes, insurance, and common area maintenance expenses attributable to the retail space.\n\n**[Other Income]{.underline}**\n\nAdditional income sources include:\n\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Category**                                                               **Annual Amount**\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Parking income (non-resident monthly parking and hourly visitor parking)   \\$108,000\n\n  Amenity fees, application fees, pet fees, and late fees                    \\$62,000\n\n  Other miscellaneous income (laundry, storage units, etc.)                  \\$28,000\n\n  **Total Other Income**                                                     **\\$198,000**\n  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**[Gross Potential Income]{.underline}**\n\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Income Component**                     **Annual Amount**\n  ---------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Residential Rental Income (normalized)   \\$5,712,000\n\n  Retail Rental Income                     \\$552,000\n\n  Other Income                             \\$198,000\n\n  **Gross Potential Income (GPI)**         **\\$6,462,000**\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**[Vacancy and Credit Loss]{.underline}**\n\nA vacancy and credit loss allowance of 5.0% of gross potential income has been applied, reflecting the current submarket vacancy of approximately 5.8% adjusted for the subject\\'s anticipated competitive advantages as a newly constructed, fully amenitized Class A property. The 5.0% rate is consistent with the stabilized vacancy levels observed at the comparable properties (ranging from 2.9% to 5.2%) and accounts for both physical vacancy and credit loss.\n\n**Vacancy and Credit Loss:** \\$6,462,000 × 5.0% = **(\\$323,100)**\n\n**[Effective Gross Income]{.underline}**\n\n**Effective Gross Income (EGI):** \\$6,462,000 − \\$323,100 = **\\$6,138,900**\n\n**[Operating Expenses]{.underline}**\n\nThe following operating expenses are projected based on the comparable operating data analyzed in the full appraisal report, industry benchmarks for Class A multifamily properties in the Research Triangle market, and the appraiser\\'s experience and professional judgment:\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Expense Category**                                  **Annual Amount**\n  ----------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Property Management (3.5% of EGI)                     \\$214,862\n\n  Real Estate Taxes (estimated)                         \\$412,000\n\n  Insurance                                             \\$148,500\n\n  Utilities (common area electric, water, sewer, gas)   \\$96,200\n\n  Repairs and Maintenance                               \\$127,500\n\n  Administrative and Payroll (on-site staff)            \\$142,000\n\n  Marketing and Advertising                             \\$38,000\n\n  Reserves for Replacement                              \\$53,000\n\n  Other Operating Expenses                              \\$30,038\n\n  **Total Operating Expenses**                          **\\$1,262,100**\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe total operating expense ratio is \\$1,262,100 ÷ \\$6,138,900 = approximately 20.6% of effective gross income. This ratio is consistent with industry norms for new-construction Class A multifamily properties, which typically operate at expense ratios between 18% and 25% of EGI, depending on location, property management structure, and amenity level.\n\n**[Net Operating Income]{.underline}**\n\n**Net Operating Income (NOI):** \\$6,138,900 − \\$1,262,100 = **\\$4,876,800**\n\nThe projected stabilized NOI of \\$4,876,800 serves as the basis for both the as-stabilized valuation (Section 6.3) and the as-completed valuation (Section 6.2).\n\n**[Section 8 --- Environmental Considerations]{.underline}**\n\nThe appraiser reviewed the Phase I Environmental Site Assessment (\\\"Phase I ESA\\\") prepared by Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC, dated October 28, 2024. The Phase I ESA was prepared by the firm\\'s lead environmental consultant, Dr. James Whitfield, P.E., in general conformance with ASTM Standard Practice E 1527-21 for Environmental Site Assessments. The Phase I ESA encompassed both parcels comprising the subject property.\n\nThe Phase I ESA identified one Recognized Environmental Condition (\\\"REC\\\") affecting the subject property: residual petroleum contamination associated with a former underground storage tank (\\\"UST\\\") located on the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 (615 Foster Street). Historical records indicate that the UST was originally installed in the 1950s in connection with the former tobacco warehouse operations and was used for the storage of heating oil. The UST had not been in active use since approximately 1988.\n\nA Phase II Environmental Site Assessment (\\\"Phase II ESA\\\") was subsequently conducted in August 2024, also by Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC. The Phase II ESA included the advancement of soil borings and the collection and laboratory analysis of soil and groundwater samples from the area surrounding the former UST location. The results of the Phase II ESA revealed petroleum hydrocarbon contamination levels in soil that exceeded the North Carolina Department of Environmental Quality (\\\"NCDEQ\\\") Inactive Hazardous Sites Branch (\\\"IHSB\\\") residential soil screening levels for certain constituents, including benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, and xylenes (\\\"BTEX\\\"). However, the detected concentrations were below the applicable NCDEQ non-residential (commercial/industrial) soil screening levels.\n\nEnvironmental remediation of the affected area was completed in September 2024. Remediation activities included the removal and proper disposal of the former UST, excavation and off-site disposal of approximately 320 cubic yards of petroleum-impacted soil, and post-remediation confirmation sampling. The remediation was performed under the oversight of NCDEQ and in accordance with an approved remediation work plan.\n\nFollowing completion of the remediation, NCDEQ issued a No Further Action (\\\"NFA\\\") letter dated September 30, 2024, for the affected area. The NFA letter confirms that no further investigation or remediation is required, subject to the following conditions:\n\n> \\(i\\) A soil vapor mitigation system must be installed and maintained in the structured parking garage and any enclosed occupied space constructed above the affected area, in accordance with specifications approved by NCDEQ; and\n>\n> \\(ii\\) Future use of the affected portion of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 --- specifically, an area encompassing approximately 0.38 acres in the northeast corner of the parcel --- is restricted to non-residential purposes. This land use restriction is memorialized in a Declaration of Land Use Restrictions recorded at the Durham County Register of Deeds.\n\nThe appraiser notes that the soil vapor mitigation system required by the NFA letter has been incorporated into the construction plans and specifications prepared by Meridian Design Collective PC and is included within the GMP contract with Graystone Construction Group Inc. The cost of the vapor mitigation system is reflected in the hard construction cost budget.\n\nThe NFA letter restricts future use of the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 (approximately 0.38 acres) to non-residential use. The appraiser notes this restriction for the record. The project plans, as reviewed by the appraiser and as described by the developer, include residential units on the upper floors of the building positioned above this area of the site. The appraiser\\'s valuation assumes that the borrower will comply with all applicable environmental laws, regulations, and use restrictions, and that any required modifications, variances, or additional remediation necessary to permit the planned use will be obtained by the borrower at the borrower\\'s cost. The appraiser expresses no opinion as to the legal permissibility of the planned residential use of the area above the environmentally restricted portion of the site. The appraiser has not adjusted the valuation conclusions to account for any potential impact of the NFA use restriction on the residential unit mix or leasability of units positioned above the restricted area.\n\nThe appraiser is not an environmental consultant and does not render opinions regarding environmental conditions, remediation adequacy, or compliance with environmental regulations. The reader is directed to the Phase I and Phase II ESA reports prepared by Sentinel Environmental Consulting LLC for a complete discussion of environmental matters.\n\n**[Section 9 --- Extraordinary Assumptions and Hypothetical Conditions]{.underline}**\n\nThe value conclusions set forth in this appraisal are subject to the following extraordinary assumptions, hypothetical conditions, and general assumptions and limiting conditions. If any extraordinary assumption is found to be false, the value conclusions may be affected.\n\n**[Extraordinary Assumptions]{.underline}**\n\n> 1\\. The appraiser assumes that the proposed development will be constructed substantially in accordance with the plans and specifications reviewed by the appraiser, including the project description, residential unit mix, retail configuration, parking allocation, and amenity package as described in Section 3 of this report. Material deviations from the reviewed plans could affect the value conclusions.\n>\n> 2\\. The appraiser assumes that the construction of the proposed development will be completed within the projected timeline, with substantial completion achieved on or before August 31, 2027, and that total project costs will not materially exceed the stated budget of \\$56,700,000.\n>\n> 3\\. The appraiser assumes that the borrower will obtain all necessary permits, approvals, and governmental authorizations required for the proposed development, including but not limited to building permits, certificates of occupancy, and any environmental approvals or variances necessary for the intended use of all portions of the property.\n>\n> 4\\. The appraiser assumes that the recorded deed of trust in favor of Triangle Commercial Bank (original amount \\$3,400,000) will be satisfied and released prior to or concurrently with the closing of the proposed construction financing from Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A.\n>\n> 5\\. The appraiser assumes that the existing encumbrances of record --- including the 20-foot utility easement benefiting Duke Energy Carolinas, the City of Durham stormwater management easement (Book 7421, Page 212), and the Declaration of Covenants, Conditions, and Restrictions recorded by the Brightleaf Historic District Association, Inc. (Book 6984, Page 1155) --- do not materially impair the development potential or market value of the property.\n>\n> 6\\. The appraiser assumes that the soil vapor mitigation system required by the NCDEQ No Further Action letter dated September 30, 2024, will be properly designed, installed, and maintained in accordance with approved specifications, and that the environmental condition of the property will not materially change from the conditions described in the Phase I and Phase II Environmental Site Assessments.\n>\n> 7\\. The appraiser assumes that the borrower will comply with all environmental use restrictions set forth in the NFA letter dated September 30, 2024, including the restriction of the northeast corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 (approximately 0.38 acres) to non-residential use, or will obtain any necessary modifications, variances, or additional approvals from NCDEQ or other governmental authorities to permit the proposed use of all portions of the property as planned in the reviewed development program.\n\n**[Hypothetical Conditions]{.underline}**\n\n> 1\\. The \\\"as-completed\\\" and \\\"as-stabilized\\\" valuations set forth in this report are based on the hypothetical condition that the proposed development has been completed as described in Section 3 and, in the case of the as-stabilized value, that stabilized occupancy of 95% has been achieved across all residential and retail units. These are prospective value opinions based on conditions that do not yet exist as of the effective date of the appraisal.\n>\n> 2\\. Market conditions, including rental rates, vacancy levels, capitalization rates, and absorption rates, are assumed to remain generally consistent with conditions observed as of the effective date of the appraisal. The appraiser has not attempted to predict future changes in market conditions.\n\n**[General Assumptions and Limiting Conditions]{.underline}**\n\n> 1\\. The appraiser assumes no responsibility for matters of a legal nature that may affect the property or the title thereto. Title to the property is assumed to be good and marketable unless otherwise stated.\n>\n> 2\\. The property is appraised free and clear of all liens and encumbrances, except as specifically noted herein.\n>\n> 3\\. The appraiser assumes that there are no hidden or unapparent conditions of the property, subsoil, or structures that would render it more or less valuable than otherwise apparent.\n>\n> 4\\. Information, estimates, and opinions furnished to the appraiser and contained in this report have been obtained from sources considered reliable and are believed to be true and correct. However, the appraiser assumes no responsibility for the accuracy of such items furnished by other parties.\n>\n> 5\\. The appraiser is not required to provide testimony or attend court by reason of this appraisal unless prior arrangements have been made.\n>\n> 6\\. The distribution of the total valuation between land and improvements, if any, applies only under the program of utilization stated herein. The separate allocations are not valid if used in conjunction with any other program of utilization.\n>\n> 7\\. The appraiser has no present or prospective interest in the subject property and no personal interest or bias with respect to the parties involved.\n>\n> 8\\. This appraisal is to be used in whole and not in part. Any use of this report that is partial or taken out of context is unauthorized and may lead to erroneous conclusions.\n\n**[Section 10 --- Valuation Summary and Certification]{.underline}**\n\n**[Valuation Summary]{.underline}**\n\nThe concluded market values of the subject property under each valuation scenario are summarized in the table below:\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Valuation Scenario**                           **Effective Date**                **Concluded Market Value**\n  ------------------------------------------------ --------------------------------- ----------------------------\n  As-Is (Land Value)                               November 15, 2024                 **\\$8,900,000**\n\n  As-Completed (Upon Completion of Construction)   Prospective --- August 31, 2027   **\\$72,800,000**\n\n  As-Stabilized (At 95% Occupancy)                 Prospective --- May 2028          **\\$76,200,000**\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**[Key Valuation Metrics]{.underline}**\n\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Metric**                                     **Value**\n  ---------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Implied Capitalization Rate (As-Stabilized)    6.40%\n\n  Price Per Residential Unit (As-Completed)      \\$343,396\n\n  Price Per Residential Unit (As-Stabilized)     \\$359,434\n\n  Price Per Square Foot of GBA (As-Completed)    \\$253.22\n\n  Price Per Square Foot of GBA (As-Stabilized)   \\$265.04\n\n  Stabilized Net Operating Income                \\$4,876,800\n\n  Operating Expense Ratio                        20.6% of EGI\n  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**[Loan Metrics]{.underline}**\n\nThe following loan metrics are provided for the lender\\'s reference and convenience only. The appraiser does not render opinions on loan underwriting, credit decisions, or the appropriateness of any financing terms.\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Metric**                                            **Value**\n  ----------------------------------------------------- -----------------------------------\n  Proposed Senior Loan Amount                           \\$47,500,000\n\n  Loan-to-Value (As-Completed)                          65.25%\n\n  Loan-to-Value (As-Stabilized)                         62.34%\n\n  Projected DSCR (Stabilized, Interest-Only at 7.85%)   1.31x\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nThe projected debt service coverage ratio is calculated on an interest-only basis at an assumed interest rate of 7.85%, applied to the proposed senior loan amount of \\$47,500,000, yielding annual debt service of \\$3,728,750. The stabilized NOI of \\$4,876,800 divided by the annual debt service produces a coverage ratio of approximately 1.31x. This metric is provided as information only; the appraiser does not express an opinion as to the adequacy or appropriateness of the projected debt service coverage in the context of the lender\\'s underwriting criteria.\n\n**[Certification]{.underline}**\n\nI certify that, to the best of my knowledge and belief:\n\n> 1\\. The statements of fact contained in this report are true and correct.\n>\n> 2\\. The reported analyses, opinions, and conclusions are limited only by the reported assumptions and limiting conditions, and are my personal, impartial, and unbiased professional analyses, opinions, and conclusions.\n>\n> 3\\. I have no present or prospective interest in the property that is the subject of this report, and I have no personal interest or bias with respect to the parties involved.\n>\n> 4\\. My engagement in this assignment was not contingent upon developing or reporting predetermined results.\n>\n> 5\\. My compensation for completing this assignment is not contingent upon the development or reporting of a predetermined value or direction in value that favors the cause of the client, the amount of the value opinion, the attainment of a stipulated result, or the occurrence of a subsequent event directly related to the intended use of this appraisal.\n>\n> 6\\. The reported analyses, opinions, and conclusions were developed, and this report has been prepared, in conformity with the Uniform Standards of Professional Appraisal Practice.\n>\n> 7\\. I have made a personal inspection of the property that is the subject of this report. The property was inspected on November 12, 2024.\n>\n> 8\\. No one provided significant real property appraisal assistance to the person signing this certification.\n>\n> 9\\. I have the knowledge and experience to competently complete this appraisal assignment.\n>\n> 10\\. This appraisal has been prepared in compliance with Title XI of the Financial Institutions Reform, Recovery, and Enforcement Act of 1989 (FIRREA), as amended.\n\nRIDGELINE APPRAISAL SERVICES INC.\n\nBy: **\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**\n\nLinda M. Haverford, MAI NC State Certified General Real Estate Appraiser License No. A-8291\n\nDate: November 15, 2024\n\n**[Appendix A --- Subject Property Photographs]{.underline}**\n\nPhotographs of the subject property as of the date of inspection (November 12, 2024) are included in the full appraisal report at Appendix A thereof. This executive summary includes representative photographs of the following views:\n\n> 1\\. Aerial view of the combined parcels (601 and 615 Foster Street) showing the cleared and rough-graded site.\n>\n> 2\\. Street-level view from Foster Street looking northeast across the subject site.\n>\n> 3\\. View of the northeastern corner of Parcel 0821-04-72-4102 showing the remediated former underground storage tank area.\n>\n> 4\\. View of the southern portion of the site showing existing stormwater management infrastructure and the approximate location of the stormwater management easement.\n>\n> 5\\. Architectural rendering of the proposed development as viewed from the intersection of Foster Street and Parrish Street (rendering provided by Meridian Design Collective PC).\n\n*\\[Photographs omitted from this executive summary document. See full appraisal report.\\]*\n\n**[Appendix B --- Comparable Data Summary]{.underline}**\n\nDetailed comparable data sheets for land sales, improved property sales, and rental comparables referenced in this executive summary are included in the full appraisal report at Appendices C through E thereof. The summary tables below are reproduced for convenience of reference.\n\n**[Land Sale Comparables]{.underline}**\n\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Sale**   **Location**                   **Date of Sale**   **Size (Acres)**   **Sale Price**   **Price/Acre**   **Adjusted Price/Acre**\n  ---------- ------------------------------ ------------------ ------------------ ---------------- ---------------- -------------------------\n  1          West Chapel Hill St., Durham   July 2024          2.81               \\$7,447,000      \\$2,650,000      \\$2,510,000\n\n  2          Morris St., Durham             April 2024         1.95               \\$5,460,000      \\$2,800,000      \\$2,620,000\n\n  3          Mangum St., Durham             January 2024       4.12               \\$9,888,000      \\$2,400,000      \\$2,550,000\n\n  4          Corporation St., Durham        September 2023     3.05               \\$7,168,000      \\$2,350,000      \\$2,480,000\n  -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Selected Rate:** \\$2,565,000/acre \\| **Subject Size:** 3.47 acres \\| **Concluded As-Is Value:** \\$8,900,000\n\n**[Rental Comparables]{.underline}**\n\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n  **Comparable**   **Property Name**                     **Location**          **Year Built**   **Units**   **Avg. Rent/Mo.**   **Occupancy**\n  ---------------- ------------------------------------- --------------------- ---------------- ----------- ------------------- ---------------\n  1                The Bellweather at American Tobacco   318 Blackwell St.     2021             186         \\$2,310             96.2%\n\n  2                Warehouse Lofts on Rigsbee            205 N. Rigsbee Ave.   2020             142         \\$2,175             94.8%\n\n  3                District on Main                      411 W. Main St.       2022             224         \\$2,245             95.5%\n\n  4                Foster Street Station                 515 Foster St.        2019             108         \\$2,095             97.1%\n  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Subject Projected Weighted Average Rent:** \\$2,245/month\n\n**[Appendix C --- Qualifications of the Appraiser]{.underline}**\n\n**[Linda M. Haverford, MAI, CRE]{.underline}**\n\n**Professional Designations:**\n\n> • Member, Appraisal Institute (MAI designation awarded 2009)\n>\n> • Member, Counselors of Real Estate (CRE)\n\n**Licensure:**\n\n> • North Carolina State Certified General Real Estate Appraiser, License No. A-8291 (active, in good standing)\n\n**Education:**\n\n> • Bachelor of Science in Real Estate, University of North Carolina at Greensboro\n>\n> • Master of Business Administration, Wake Forest University School of Business\n>\n> • Completion of all Appraisal Institute continuing education requirements\n\n**Professional Experience:**\n\nMs. Haverford has over 24 years of experience in commercial real estate appraisal, specializing in the valuation of multifamily, mixed-use, and commercial development properties throughout the Research Triangle region and greater North Carolina. Her experience encompasses the full range of appraisal assignments, including market value appraisals for acquisition and construction financing, portfolio valuations, litigation support, and consulting assignments for institutional lenders, developers, and government agencies.\n\nMs. Haverford is the principal and founder of Ridgeline Appraisal Services Inc., an independent appraisal firm based in Raleigh, North Carolina. Established in 2006, Ridgeline Appraisal Services serves institutional lenders, commercial banks, developers, private equity firms, and governmental agencies throughout the southeastern United States. The firm specializes in complex multifamily, mixed-use, and commercial real estate valuations.\n\n**Publications and Speaking:**\n\nMs. Haverford is a published author and frequent speaker on multifamily valuation topics, with articles appearing in *The Appraisal Journal* and *Real Estate Issues*. She ha...(truncated)", "tool_call_id": "call_00_opKRmN2ONWWX7NiT0FGx6196", "tool_name": "read", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"documents/appraisal-summary.docx\"}"}
