{"turn": 81, "role": "tool", "result": "== pdf/la_formatted.pdf\n1:CONSTRUCTION-TO-MINI-PERMANENT\n2:LOAN AGREEMENT\n3:by and among\n4:PINNACLE SAVINGS BANK, N.A., as Lender,\n5:HARBORVIEW CAPITAL PARTNERS LLC, as Borrower,\n6:ELENA VASQUEZ-TORRES and REGINALD K. OSEI, as Guarantors\n7:\n8:Dated as of [________], 2025\n9:The Piedmont at Brightleaf\n10:601 and 615 Foster Street, Durham, North Carolina 27701\n== pdfm/memo_formatted.pdf\n1:PRIVILEGED AND CONFIDENTIAL — ATTORNEY–CLIENT COMMUNICATION — ATTORNEY\n2:WORK PRODUCT — PREPARED IN ANTICIPATION OF LOAN DOCUMENTATION\n3:\n4:MEMORANDUM\n5:TO:\n6:\n7:David Forsythe, Senior Vice President — Commercial Real Estate Lending,\n8:Pinnacle Savings Bank, N.A.; Senior Loan Committee (copy)\n9:\n10:FROM:\n--- memo headings page\n                         Privileged & Confidential — Attorney–Client Communication — Attorney Work Product\n\n\n\nwritten would advance $3,475,000 more to hard costs than the guaranteed\nmaximum price authorises.\n\n6. Detailed issues and recommended resolutions\n\nA. Loan sizing and the capital structure\n\nISSUE 1 — The Loan breaches the Term Sheet’s loan-to-cost covenant\n(High).\nSource. Term Sheet §§3.1 (loan amount $47,500,000) and 6.1 (LTC not to exceed\n80% of Total Project Costs, stated to be $56,700,000); CM §§5.1, 5.3 and 6.1\n(identifies an 83.77% LTC and a $2,140,000 shortfall).\nAnalysis. $47,500,000 ÷ $56,700,000 = 83.7743%. The Bank executed a term\nsheet containing a covenant that its own loan amount breaches. The difficulty is\nnot the identification of the problem (the CM identifies it) but the proposed cure:\nthe Bank’s condition is a $2,140,000 cash deposit into a Bank-controlled equity\nescrow “applied to project costs first, ahead of loan advances, to bring the effective\nLTC into compliance” (CM §§5.3, 10.1.2). Depositing cash does not change either\nthe numerator or the denominator of the ratio. The cure works only if the amount\nadvanced under the senior loan is correspondin