{"turn": 90, "role": "tool", "result": "Pages:           29\n                         Privileged & Confidential — Attorney–Client Communication — Attorney Work Product\n\n\n\nnet operating income”); CM §11(c) (extension test at 1.15x on trailing three-month\nannualised NOI).\nAnalysis. At the commencement of the mini-perm period the Project will have just\nreached substantial completion and will have no twelve-month operating history,\nso the stated test cannot be performed on its own terms. The Term Sheet also does\nnot define NOI, whether replacement reserves and the CC&R assessment are\ndeducted, or how interest is computed for the test (contract rate versus stressed\nrate).\nRecommendation. Adopt a two-stage test: a projected test at the commencement of\nthe mini-perm period using the Appraisal’s concluded stabilized NOI, and a trailing\ntest once twelve months of history exist (with a trailing three-month annualised\ntest in the interim), together with a defined NOI (Draft §1.1 deducts a $250 per\nunit replacement reserve and the CC&R assessment) and the cure mechanics in\nDraft §8.3(b).\n\nISSUE 28 — Minor Term Sheet internal inconsistencies to correct in the\nclosing checklist (Low).\nSource and analysis. (a) Term Sheet §5.1 records “Borrower Equity (land\npreviously acquired; see Uses below) $0” while §5.2 records the $8,200,000 land\ncost as a use — a self-contradiction that is the arithmetic root of Issue 3. (b) Term\nSheet §11 requires “payment of the origination fee in the amount of $475,000” as a\ncondition precedent to the initial advance while §3.4 permits the fee to be funded\nfrom loan proceeds — a circular condition. (c) Term Sheet §22 calls the $75,000\nexpense deposit “non-refundable” but then provides for the return of any unused\nportion; and the record contains no evidence that the deposit was paid. (d) Term\nSheet §15 contains no cure period for a breach of the single-purpose entity\ncovenant even though the same section provides cure periods for lesser breaches.\nRecommendation. Correct (a) and (b) in the documentation (Draft §§2.2, 2.9(a),\n3.11 and Schedule 1.1 handle the funding point); confirm receipt of the $75,000\ndeposit; and consider whether the SPE covenant should carry a short cure period\n(Draft §10.10 deliberately does not).\n\nH. Documentation and file integrity\n\nISSUE 29 — The approval record is incomplete (Medium).\nSource. CM cover page (“Reviewed By: ______, Senior Credit Officer”) and §14.2\n(all three signature blocks blank); Term Sheet §4 (“reviewed by the Lender’s\ninternal appraisal review department”) — no review memorandum in the record.\nAnalysis. The loan is recommended for approval but not evidenced as approved,\nand the appraisal review on which the FIRREA compliance statement depends is\nnot in the file. Both should be in place before closing; the appraisal review is also a\nsupervisory expectation for a $47,500,000 commercial real estate transaction.\nRecommendation. Obtain the executed approval and the appraisal review\nmemorandum, and add the review memorandum to the closing deliverables (Draft\n§3.4(a)).\n\n", "tool_call_id": "call_00_ET_GAzjsUt7o2gVtTCxZ8Eh6286", "tool_name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd $WORKSPACE_DIR/work && rm -f pdfm/memo_formatted.pdf && timeout 240 soffice --headless --convert-to pdf memo_formatted.docx --outdir pdfm >/dev/null 2>&1; pdfinfo pdfm/memo_formatted.pdf|grep Pages; pdftotext -layout -f 24 -l 25 pdfm/memo_formatted.pdf - | head -50\"}"}
