{"turn": 106, "role": "tool", "result": "    (c) Application. All prepayments shall be applied first to accrued and unpaid\n        interest and fees, then to principal.\n    (d) Permitted Refinancing. Notwithstanding Section 7.3, Borrower may\n        prepay the Loan in full in connection with a refinancing of the Loan with\n        Lender or an Affiliate of Lender, or with a third-party lender, provided that\n        (i) no Event of Default exists, (ii) the refinancing repays the Obligations in\n        full concurrently with the closing of the refinancing, and (iii) Lender has\n        consented to the refinancing in writing (which consent shall not be\n        unreasonably withheld where the refinancing repays the Loan in full and\n=== 8.4\n8.4 Borrower Net Worth. Borrower shall maintain Borrower Net Worth of not\nless than $12,000,000, tested annually within one hundred twenty (120) days after\nthe end of Borrower’s fiscal year and certified in a Compliance Certificate. [Note to\nDraft: The $12,000,000 threshold is taken from Section 6.4 of the Term Sheet, but\nit appears unachievable on a book basis: immediately after Closing, Borrower’s\nassets (land at $8,200,000 plus construction in progress funded by Advances) and\nits liabilities (the Loan and the Mezzanine Loan, aggregating the same amount)\nproduce book equity of approximately zero, and even with a $2,140,000 Equity\nDeposit the resulting equity is far below $12,000,000. Lender must elect one of the\nfollowing before execution: (i) accept the fair-value-based definition of “Borrower\nNet Worth” set forth in Section 1.1; (ii) reset the threshold to an achievable level\n", "tool_call_id": "call_00_I4TEZRBAMjFeTzDIgfKr0768", "tool_name": "bash", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cd /tmp/chk && pdftotext -layout loan-agreement-draft.pdf - | grep -B2 -A6 'Permitted Refinancing' | head -14; echo \\\"=== 8.4\\\"; pdftotext -layout loan-agreement-draft.pdf - | grep -A10 '8.4 Borrower Net Worth' | head -14\"}"}
{"turn": 107, "role": "assistant", "text": "Both deliverables are complete and validated. Let me write the summary response.", "tool_calls": [{"id": "call_00_ET_nLVErl35QM0p37mktIZn8665", "name": "write", "arguments": "{\"file_path\": \"response.md\", \"content\": \"# Deliverables\\n\\n| File | Description | Length |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `loan-agreement-draft.docx` | Draft No. 1 Construction-to-Mini-Permanent Loan Agreement (Lender's counsel draft), with 16 articles, 5 schedules and 2 exhibits | 69 pages / ~28,900 words |\\n| `cover-memorandum.docx` | Cover memorandum: top-risk register, arithmetic verification, 30 detailed issues with recommended resolutions, diligence gaps, bracketed items and next steps | 29 pages / ~13,100 words |\\n\\n# What I did\\n\\n**1. Inventoried and read the full record.** Seven documents: executed Term Sheet (Jan 10, 2025), Senior Loan Committee Credit Memorandum (Jan 22, 2025), Appraisal executive summary, Preliminary Title Commitment (NC-2024-88712), Phase I ESA excerpt, Borrower organizational chart / operating-agreement excerpts, and the construction budget and draw schedule workbook. Every material proposition in the deliverables is tied to a specific document and section.\\n\\n**2. Recomputed every figure independently** rather than relying on the totals in the sources. This surfaced errors that the record itself does not catch — for example, the 10% retainage is $3,475,000 (not the $3,275,000 scheduled, which is 9.42%); the draw schedule's column components total $48,500,000 against a $47,500,000 commitment; the interest reserve is short by ~$361,500 at the Credit Memorandum's own 4.35% SOFR assumption (~$1,047,000 at +100 bps), with the Credit Memorandum's $565,000 estimate itself overstated by ~$203,000; 212 × $2,245 × 12 = $5,711,280, not $5,712,000; the hard-cost contingency is 3.28%, not the 4.14% stated; and the mezzanine loan's pay-in-kind interest accretes to ~$12.55M, taking combined leverage to ~82.5% of as-completed value.\\n\\n**3. Drafted the agreement from the verified record, repairing the mechanics.** The agreement implements the Term Sheet and the Credit Memorandum's conditions of approval, and fixes what the sources get wrong: an 80% loan-to-cost borrowing base that caps advances at $45,360,000 and cures both the LTC and the 65% LTV breaches; a corrected net-of-retainage draw schedule reconciled to the commitment; a full set of NFA covenants covering all four conditions (not just the vapour system); single-purpose-entity, independent-manager and Article 8 pledge mechanics; SOFR benchmark-replacement language; and North Carolina foreclosure, fair-value and usury provisions.\\n\\n**4. Flagged, rather than papered over, what the record cannot resolve.** Twelve italicised *Note to Draft* items in the agreement, each cross-referenced to the numbered issue in the memorandum, plus a §7.2 list of the business decisions the Bank must make before execution.\\n\\n# The ten closing-critical findings\\n\\n1. **The Loan breaches the Term Sheet's own 80% LTC covenant** (83.77%; $2,140,000 over) — and the Credit Memorandum's equity-escrow cure does not change the ratio arithmetic unless advances are capped.\\n2. **It also breaches the 65% as-completed LTV ceiling** by $180,000; the Credit Memorandum tests against a 70% \\\"policy\\\" figure and reports \\\"Pass.\\\"\\n3. **There is no genuine equity.** The sources and uses funds all $56.7M — including the $8.2M land cost already paid in 2023 — with debt, so the mezzanine loan can only be reimbursing the land. The Credit Memorandum's \\\"18.2% equity\\\" and \\\"14.2% HVCRE\\\" figures double-count the land; the real contributed capital is ~2.9%.\\n4. **The equity escrow is circular** if applied to the developer fee — resolved in the draft by allocating it to hard/soft costs only.\\n5. **A $3.4M Triangle Commercial Bank deed of trust is unsatisfied, unquantified and unbudgeted**, with no source of funds in the uses.\\n6. **The NCDEQ non-residential use restriction conflicts with the residential programme**, and the \\\"14 units\\\" the Credit Memorandum quantifies is unsourced (it equals the three-bedroom count; the ESA expressly declines to verify).\\n7. **The deed notice required by NFA Condition 4 (due Nov 29, 2024) appears unrecorded** and is absent from Schedule B-II — although the Appraisal says it was recorded.\\n8. **The membership-interest pledge is promised to two lenders at once**, with no Article 8 election or priority mechanism.\\n9. **The $12,000,000 Borrower net worth covenant is unachievable** — post-closing book equity is ~$0, or $2.14M with the equity deposit.\\n10. **The 75% supermajority gives the mezzanine lender's affiliate a veto over any refinancing over $1,000,000** — i.e. over the Bank's own exit.\\n\\nAlso flagged: a 70%-vs-65% LTV conflict, a $11.875M-vs-$23.75M guaranty ambiguity, a cross-reference to a guarantor net worth threshold that does not exist, mechanics'-lien relation-back exposure from the 2024 demolition and remediation, parcel acreage and site-orientation conflicts between the ESA and the title record, ARC approval assumed by the Appraiser but not evidenced, a $34,500/yr CC&R assessment missing from NOI, two blank pages' worth of unbudgeted carrying costs, an unsigned credit approval, and the Credit Memorandum's inaccurate description of North Carolina as a judicial foreclosure state.\\n\\n# Validation\\n\\nBoth files pass ECMA-376 schema validation, render cleanly (69 and 29 pages), and contain no residual markdown artefacts, blank pages or dangling cross-references. All \\\"Cover Memorandum, Issue N\\\" references in the agreement were reconciled against the memorandum's final issue numbering, and the memorandum's risk register uses those same numbers.\\n\"}"}], "input_tokens": 353646, "output_tokens": 1381, "finish_reason": "completed"}
